WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.070
Tim Ferriss: Hello, boys and girls, ladies
and germs.

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:18.070
This is Tim Ferriss.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:21.750
Welcome to another episode of The Tim Ferriss
Show, where it is my job to deconstruct world

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.710
class performers across many, many disciplines.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:28.030
My guest today, I'm so happy to have him,
is Bill Gurley.

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.180
You can find him on Twitter @bgurley.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.290
That's G-U-R-L-E-Y.

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:35.990
Bill has spent more than 20 years as a general
partner at Benchmark.

00:00:35.990 --> 00:00:39.310
Before entering the venture capital business,
Bill spent four years on Wall Street as a

00:00:39.310 --> 00:00:42.125
top ranked research analyst, including three
years at Credit — got a little Elmer Fudd,

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:43.700
hold on, including three years at Credit Suisse
First Boston.

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:47.530
Bill also maintains a blog on the evolution
and economics of high-technology businesses

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:51.860
called Above the Crowd, which you can find
at abovethecrowd.com.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:57.010
Over his venture career, he has worked with
such companies as GrubHub, Next door, OpenTable,

00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:00.390
Stitch Fix, Uber and Zillow among many others.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.269
Bill has BS in computer science from the University
of Florida and an MBA from the University

00:01:04.269 --> 00:01:05.310
of Texas.

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:07.260
He's also chartered financial analyst.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.380
Bill is a board trustee at the Santa Fe Institute,
a research and education center focused on

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:15.170
the study and understanding of complex adaptive
systems.

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:17.260
Bill, nice to see you.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:18.260
Thanks for making the time.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:19.260
Bill Gurley: Thanks for having me.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.920
Tim Ferriss: All right, so I wanted to start
with a number of things that I've seen pop

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.200
up repeatedly in various forms in doing prep
for this conversation.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:36.420
And the first I wanted to get your take on
and expansion on is Michael Porter's book

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:41.560
Competitive Strategy: Techniques for Analyzing
Industries and Competitors, which you have

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:47.640
described I believe as the most efficient
short-form MBA that one can find.

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:53.410
Could you please explain what this book is
and if you still hold that opinion, why you

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:54.440
hold that opinion?

00:01:54.440 --> 00:02:00.280
Bill Gurley: If you do go to get an MBA like
in the first semester or whatever, they usually

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.229
make you take a corporate strategy course
and it's the first book assigned.

00:02:04.229 --> 00:02:08.099
It's like extremely well known business book.

00:02:08.099 --> 00:02:16.250
There's a belief in Silicon Valley that an
MBA's worthless and that people shit on it

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:17.730
all the time.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:24.770
Yet, I would say 80 percent or 90 percent
of the entrepreneurs I meet would benefit

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:28.640
greatly by reading the first three chapters
of this book.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:34.500
And it's really just about trying to understand
the dynamics of industry.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:41.519
One of the most common mistakes entrepreneurs
make is they come up with some kind of technological

00:02:41.519 --> 00:02:47.310
breakthrough in their own mind, but they don't
spend any time analyzing the industry structure

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:51.390
or whether the go-to market is going to be
possible or not.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:56.560
And the book just has a wonderful framework
for thinking about the competitive dynamics

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.030
of an industry and whether or not you'd be
able to break in or be successful or maintain

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:03.030
success.

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:10.170
Tim Ferriss: Well, this actually makes me
think more broadly of frameworks and recipes

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.890
as it were for making decisions.

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:18.250
So back in the day, you were a sell-side analyst
and I would love for you to define what that

00:03:18.250 --> 00:03:22.209
is and also just define buy-side versus sell-side.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:29.110
And at that time, at one point at least, "...the
top three analysts covering the PC industry

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:30.110
retired…"

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:36.400
And I'm reading from a transcript on Medium,
"...Dan Benton, David Korus…" if I'm pronouncing

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.400
that —
Bill Gurley: That's correct.

00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:41.819
Tim Ferriss: "…and Charlie Wolf…" and
then "...Charlie became the advisor for young

00:03:41.819 --> 00:03:42.819
analysts…"

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:43.819
so you got to work with him.

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:44.830
Here's the part that's of interest to me.

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:48.959
So "Dan and David became friends, so they
both gave me all their [financial] models."

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:51.370
And I'm curious what they gave you.

00:03:51.370 --> 00:03:53.739
What were those models?

00:03:53.739 --> 00:03:56.330
And do you still use any of those models?

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:57.730
Bill Gurley: No, no, no.

00:03:57.730 --> 00:04:01.620
So to be specific, but let me start with the
sell versus buy.

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:06.930
So and the industry's changed over the years,
but a sell-side analyst is someone who does

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:14.180
research on behalf of an investment bank that
is presumably going to make money from trading

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:19.090
on their trading desk that someone sends your
way because they valued the research.

00:04:19.090 --> 00:04:22.650
So you're providing research to help sell
stocks.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.510
That's how you're going to get paid.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:28.750
The buy-side is anyone at a mutual fund who's
trading for their own account.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.669
So if you're a buy-side analyst, you don't
publish.

00:04:31.669 --> 00:04:36.590
It doesn't get public because you're using
that as proprietary information.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:40.510
The sell-side stuff gets broadcast broadly.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:45.280
And usually when someone changes estimates
or you hear about a ratings change, that's

00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:49.840
usually a sell-side analyst that has made
a decision to change something.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:51.180
And that's what ends up on CNBC.

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:56.100
Tim Ferriss: And so a sell-side analyst, this
is going to sound very naive, but a sell-side

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:59.170
analyst could make a recommendation to buy,
right?

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:02.530
They could make a recommendation like a strong
buy recommendation or a strong sell or somewhere

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:03.889
in between the two.

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:07.692
That's I think where my Long Island brain
was having trouble grasping the basics.

00:05:07.692 --> 00:05:11.389
Bill Gurley: There's a controversy about that
because the bank makes money in multiple ways

00:05:11.389 --> 00:05:13.790
and they question whether people are biased
or not.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:19.280
And then you had the whole Henry Blodget thing
from the 2000 period.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.280
So it's complicated.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.600
Tim Ferriss: And could you describe, I know
that you don't at this point use them any

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.680
longer, but the company-specific models, what
were the models [inaudible]?

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:34.890
Bill Gurley: I was super fortunate my entire
career by being in places where windows opened

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.170
up that I was able to take advantage of.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:42.780
But the situation you described, I landed
on Wall Street very fortunately, started covering

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:48.350
an industry, and there's a magazine called
Institutional Investor that pulls the buy-side

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:53.470
and ranks the analyst and the top three analyst
in the category I entered all retired within

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:54.560
one year.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.590
And they all became friends partially from
my own networking, like I was reaching out

00:05:59.590 --> 00:06:03.509
to them, but the models they shared were the
models they had built on the companies we

00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:04.920
all covered.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:10.690
So their own particular version of Dell Financials
or Compaq or Microsoft.

00:06:10.690 --> 00:06:13.419
And so it just helped me have a better understanding.

00:06:13.419 --> 00:06:15.910
I could see what they did, that kind of thing.

00:06:15.910 --> 00:06:16.920
Tim Ferriss: Got it.

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.540
So what I'm trying to unpack also through
the course of this conversation is just how

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.090
you think about models and making decisions.

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:25.819
I find that interesting.

00:06:25.819 --> 00:06:31.230
So this is hopping around a little bit, but
you did not pursue Google as an investment

00:06:31.230 --> 00:06:37.910
in 2002 and you had some investing rules of
thumb in place.

00:06:37.910 --> 00:06:43.760
And I suppose what I'm wondering is how you
think about rules as an investor in the sense

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:49.160
that it seems like perhaps to be consistently
great you need rules, but if you have rules,

00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:51.240
you're inevitably going to miss some great
opportunities.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.070
But perhaps that's okay.

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:56.069
How do you think about rules?

00:06:56.069 --> 00:07:02.740
Was missing Google the result of a flaw in
the set of rules that you had at the time?

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.680
Or was it just sort of a collateral miss that
was —

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.110
Bill Gurley: Well, let me work my way back
to it because it's clearly the biggest mistake

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:12.110
of my career.

00:07:12.110 --> 00:07:15.400
So I thought about it a lot.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:22.639
So I think any investor starts with just building
bedrock and that comes from reading.

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:26.569
And there's a ton of books in history.

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:31.009
You can go through all the Buffett letters
as an example.

00:07:31.009 --> 00:07:34.100
You can read Peter Lynch's One Up On Wall
Street.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:38.060
There's my good friend Mike Mauboussin who
has put out some amazing books that are a

00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:41.069
lot more nuanced about stock prices, that
kind of thing.

00:07:41.069 --> 00:07:44.630
A Random Walk Down Wall Street by Burton Malkiel.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:51.850
And then you build your bedrock and then you're
out there and you're looking for an opportunity.

00:07:51.850 --> 00:07:55.250
So I think public stocks are a little different
than venture, but there's some overlap.

00:07:55.250 --> 00:08:00.440
But you're looking for a reality that you
think is going to emerge.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:02.160
It's not priced into the stock.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:07.440
So that requires you both to know or to think
you know where the world's going.

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:12.290
And also to know what the expectations are
currently embedded in the stock because if

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:16.759
you just have the same opinion that's already
embedded, you're not going to make any money.

00:08:16.759 --> 00:08:23.389
There's a great piece by Howard Marks where
he talks about you have to be right and contrarian.

00:08:23.389 --> 00:08:25.349
Yeah, you can't just be right.

00:08:25.349 --> 00:08:29.500
You have to be right and contrarian, and that's
more difficult.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:33.959
There's a famous saying that I'm sure it's
been uttered on your podcast before, but kind

00:08:33.959 --> 00:08:35.650
of strong opinions, loosely held.

00:08:35.650 --> 00:08:36.850
Tim Ferriss: Yes.

00:08:36.850 --> 00:08:41.849
Bill Gurley: I think all investors have to
work within that framework because things

00:08:41.849 --> 00:08:42.849
change.

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:44.200
There's many, many variables.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.440
None of them are constant, they're all dynamic.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:55.800
And the minute you set a very hard rule, then
you might be setting yourself up for a mistake.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:01.550
And venture, I have found, is a world where
that happens frequently.

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:07.830
And so the Google example, and it's really
important to say that we didn't lay chase.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:13.920
There were 25 employees Larry and Sergey presented,
they presented to I'm sure a whole bunch of

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:14.920
firms.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:15.920
And we didn't lay chase.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:17.660
We should have laid chase, but we didn't.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.980
At the time, Yahoo was at $10 down from, like,
80.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.790
Excite was going bankrupt.

00:09:24.790 --> 00:09:30.279
So search didn't look that exciting from an
external viewpoint.

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:34.760
You had two PhD founders who had never been
a CEO before and were insistent they were

00:09:34.760 --> 00:09:38.680
going to be good as a CEO.

00:09:38.680 --> 00:09:40.550
Normally that's a red flag.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:41.550
Yeah, PhD founders.

00:09:41.550 --> 00:09:45.660
And there was just a number of things where
you would make a list and say, "Oops, this

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.050
probably isn't what you want to do."

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:53.180
Now I'd like to highlight, and I always do,
the two best venture capitalists in the world

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:59.170
at the time, John Doerr, Mike Moritz kind
of locked hands and said yes.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:06.320
And so it'd be erroneous for me to say, "Oh,
if you were a well-studied venture catalyst,

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:10.770
you obviously get to know at that point in
time because the two bests didn't."

00:10:10.770 --> 00:10:14.630
And I think it's just the subtlety of the
game.

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:19.611
There's a great reality in venture that a
lot of people talk about now, but you can

00:10:19.611 --> 00:10:21.990
only lose one time your money.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.180
And in a case like Google, you make, what?

00:10:25.180 --> 00:10:26.820
10,000 times your money.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:33.790
And that asymmetric result means you have
to bias towards positive in a situation like

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:37.370
that because the odds are just ridiculously
different.

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:42.350
So what should have happened is I remember
one of my partners looking at Larry and saying,

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:46.030
"What does it take to get this deal done right
now?"

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:50.570
Because he was a closer tie and Larry said
"120 pre."

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.690
And we should have said, "How about 150?"

00:10:52.690 --> 00:10:59.779
If I could go back, but you can't go back.

00:10:59.779 --> 00:11:01.080
Deal got done at 80.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:05.720
So below what Larry had told us, but still
obviously historic.

00:11:05.720 --> 00:11:14.830
Tim Ferriss: So how did you then revise your
rules of thumb or rules moving forward after

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:15.830
that, if at all?

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:21.640
Is a case like that granted it turned into
what Google is now, so it's easy to maybe

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:25.670
punish yourself for not laying chase.

00:11:25.670 --> 00:11:27.970
But how did things change after that?

00:11:27.970 --> 00:11:35.620
Bill Gurley: After that, the firm did Twitter,
Snapchat, Uber, we got some right.

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:37.200
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you did.

00:11:37.200 --> 00:11:43.980
Bill Gurley: And around that time I remember
we used to give a book out to our LPs, our

00:11:43.980 --> 00:11:46.820
limited partners, our investors at every annual
meeting.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:51.930
And I think Bruce had just read The Rational
Optimist, which is a Matt Ridley book.

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:57.810
And he started using a phrase at our partner
meeting, "What could go right?"

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:03.120
And because of this asymmetric outcome thing
where you could make 10,000 times your money

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:07.540
and only lose once on the downside, it was
the right frame of mind.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:15.850
So it's very easy to get into a trap in venture
where getting no right feels like a win.

00:12:15.850 --> 00:12:20.140
And it's just not that, I mean obviously you
can't do every deal, you can't do every investment,

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:21.140
you go broke.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:30.970
But getting overly jazzed about correctly
identifying a negative or a no, it's just

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:32.470
not that big a deal.

00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:33.470
It's not the job.

00:12:33.470 --> 00:12:35.130
The job is to find the outliers.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:41.160
Tim Ferriss: So my experience, granted it's
limited in some respects, my experience and

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.770
perception of Benchmark 'cause you guys are
very, very selective more, let me rephrase

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:49.010
that, more selective than a lot of venture
capital firms and a lot of angels who, maybe

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:53.779
like the poker player who doesn't mind his
or her bank roll, yes, you can only use 1X

00:12:53.779 --> 00:12:58.329
your money, but if you do lose all of your
money, the jig is up, right?

00:12:58.329 --> 00:13:01.670
Bill Gurley: That's the problem with the complete
opposite theory.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.730
You can't just do every investment you meet.

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:09.810
And we have another challenge, Tim, which
is we've for a variety of reasons have chosen

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:15.850
a strategy where we don't let our money walk
around without our work product and our involvement.

00:13:15.850 --> 00:13:21.709
And so we go on a board if we make an investment
and we usually become the largest shareholder

00:13:21.709 --> 00:13:23.230
on the board.

00:13:23.230 --> 00:13:28.890
And as a result, our limitation is our board
seats more than the capital actually.

00:13:28.890 --> 00:13:30.149
Tim Ferriss: It's a tie.

00:13:30.149 --> 00:13:34.510
Bill Gurley: You can't do 20 of them, like
not well.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:35.510
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:41.279
So you mentioned Howard Marks, Oaktree Capital,
who's been on the podcast twice.

00:13:41.279 --> 00:13:42.631
Very, very impressive fellow.

00:13:42.631 --> 00:13:47.750
So I've seen you mention two investors, I'm
sure you've mentioned more, but two have come

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:51.520
up repeatedly in various interviews and so
on.

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:53.560
Howard Marks and Stan Druckenmiller.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:57.940
Could you please speak to what makes them
impressive or interesting to you?

00:13:57.940 --> 00:14:01.790
Bill Gurley: Some time in the last four or
five years when interest rates wouldn't get

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:07.630
away from zero for so long, I told myself
I have to learn more about macro because I

00:14:07.630 --> 00:14:11.800
just know nothing and it's causing all kind
of problems in my industry.

00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:14.520
I had been reading Howard's work for years.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:20.950
One of the things I just love about people
like Howard and Buffett does it, but people

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:28.440
who archive their thought process just as
part of their own process.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:34.820
But it's quite kind if you're a learner in
the field, to have someone of that capability

00:14:34.820 --> 00:14:40.610
that insists upon writing these letters and
making them public, which they've done.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:45.220
So I started reading Howard's work when I
was on the sell-side 25 years ago and I've

00:14:45.220 --> 00:14:50.490
gotten to know him now and in addition to
being very, very bright, he's also just a

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:55.139
wonderful human and fun to interact with.

00:14:55.139 --> 00:15:01.389
There aren't many people, if you study financial
history, most people in Buffett included will

00:15:01.389 --> 00:15:04.029
tell you, "Macro is impossible.

00:15:04.029 --> 00:15:05.220
You shouldn't even try."

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:11.740
And the two individuals you mentioned are
two of the only ones that are known for being

00:15:11.740 --> 00:15:14.610
successful in macro investing.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:20.839
Howard mostly by being one of the most successful
and longest tenured investor in the bond market

00:15:20.839 --> 00:15:28.170
and Stan for taking more kind of single individual
bets that are macro in nature going back to

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:39.269
his success with Soros, he's become re-famous
in the past 12 months for predicting the inflation

00:15:39.269 --> 00:15:41.899
situation we're in and being very loud about
it.

00:15:41.899 --> 00:15:45.839
Tim Ferriss: If you don't mind, just give
a definition — it could be simple — of

00:15:45.839 --> 00:15:48.290
macro investing for people who may not know
that term.

00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:52.579
Bill Gurley: Macro, if you go to business
school, the there's two economic class you

00:15:52.579 --> 00:15:55.910
can take: microeconomics and macroeconomics.

00:15:55.910 --> 00:16:01.300
Microeconomics is a lot about what's discussed
in competitive strategy, the Michael Porter

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:02.300
book.

00:16:02.300 --> 00:16:09.630
So it's about the interaction between firms
within an industry pricing, that kind of thing,

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:10.630
competitive dynamics.

00:16:10.630 --> 00:16:14.529
Macro is the study of economies writ large.

00:16:14.529 --> 00:16:17.510
And I'm fascinated with complex systems.

00:16:17.510 --> 00:16:22.610
Our economy is certainly one of those things
as is weather and whatnot, which is why I've

00:16:22.610 --> 00:16:25.830
gotten involved with the Santa Fe Institute.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:28.940
But they're nearly impossible to predict.

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:32.200
And it's where you get into real, real trouble.

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:38.610
There might be a variable you're not tracking
that has never flipped from zero to one.

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:43.639
And when it does flip from zero to one, all
your models, all your planning are out the

00:16:43.639 --> 00:16:46.389
window because this other thing's different
this time.

00:16:46.389 --> 00:16:52.480
I mean it's why no one can predict the weather
more than five days in front of us because

00:16:52.480 --> 00:16:55.319
it is just too dynamic and too complex.

00:16:55.319 --> 00:16:57.930
And most people feel that way about macro.

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:05.220
Tim Ferriss: Let me go back in time a little
bit, revisiting the sell-side analyst side

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:06.220
of things.

00:17:06.220 --> 00:17:10.880
So I believe at one point, and I could be
getting the terminology wrong, but you are

00:17:10.880 --> 00:17:14.199
an Institutional Investor, which you already
mentioned Institutional Investor, All-American

00:17:14.199 --> 00:17:17.699
— or All-America — Research Team –
Bill Gurley: Partly because there's three

00:17:17.699 --> 00:17:19.829
people that left the field, but yes.

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:23.902
Tim Ferriss: So what allows someone to land
on that list?

00:17:23.902 --> 00:17:24.902
How do they choose the people?

00:17:24.902 --> 00:17:27.870
Bill Gurley: So it's a poll of your customers.

00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:29.900
It's a poll of the buy-side.

00:17:29.900 --> 00:17:34.730
So they literally poll the buy-side in each
industry and say, "Who was most helpful?"

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:41.280
I will tell you that, and I don't know, this
probably came from some book I read in business

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:46.370
school, but when I showed up, and this is
a framework someone could use.

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:53.480
But when I showed up on Wall Street in one
of the very first weekly meetings, they introduced

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:55.100
us to the sales force.

00:17:55.100 --> 00:18:01.060
The sales force is the individual at your
firm responsible for that account, for Fidelity,

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:05.900
for Wellington, for Teachers of Texas, or
whatever.

00:18:05.900 --> 00:18:08.240
And they own the relationship.

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:15.820
And somewhere in my youthful wisdom, I decided
to ask each salesperson, is there one client

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:22.750
that will spend 30 to 45 minutes with me as
a new analyst and just tell me what they want.

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:24.110
I'm just going to ask them questions.

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:26.190
I'm not going to have anything for them.

00:18:26.190 --> 00:18:29.409
I just want to know how I can serve them best.

00:18:29.409 --> 00:18:35.030
And I did 20 of those interviews before I
had started the job.

00:18:35.030 --> 00:18:39.420
And that's a roundabout answer to how do you
get on the list because I knew what they were

00:18:39.420 --> 00:18:41.190
looking for at that point.

00:18:41.190 --> 00:18:43.010
Tim Ferriss: So what made you a good analyst?

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:44.720
And let me explain why I'm asking.

00:18:44.720 --> 00:18:48.980
I'm asking because one of the advantages that
you bring to a board, which you've already

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:54.410
discussed to a board of a startup in the art
of company building is your in-depth understanding

00:18:54.410 --> 00:19:00.420
of how the public markets work and if a company
is aiming for going public and so on, you

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:09.309
have knowledge that intimidates or befuddles
a lot of folks who might otherwise want to

00:19:09.309 --> 00:19:10.309
be on the board.

00:19:10.309 --> 00:19:13.050
So I'm curious to know what made you a good
analyst?

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:18.680
Bill Gurley: The thing I learned in those
interviews, both in terms of the — because

00:19:18.680 --> 00:19:21.130
I would also ask him, "Who does a really good
job of this?"

00:19:21.130 --> 00:19:24.559
And then if I could, I'd try and befriend
that person.

00:19:24.559 --> 00:19:30.559
But what I heard frequently back was, look,
and this is a little different from what the

00:19:30.559 --> 00:19:37.000
world perceives, "I don't really need you
to make this buy or sell recommendation.

00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:45.809
What I would really get a huge benefit from
is if you provide a point of view or a piece

00:19:45.809 --> 00:19:51.600
of analysis that causes us to think differently
about a particular company or industry.

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:58.429
And you go off and do some work that other
people haven't thought of that causes us to

00:19:58.429 --> 00:20:03.179
question that makes us want to talk to you
and hear what you're thinking."

00:20:03.179 --> 00:20:09.890
And so that became really the essence of what
I was focused on and partially from listening

00:20:09.890 --> 00:20:16.000
to them and partially from mimicking and copying
David Korus, who you mentioned, I started

00:20:16.000 --> 00:20:25.990
doing a weekly fax at the time and David was
doing that before he quit.

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:28.190
And so I started doing it too.

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:38.169
And interestingly, the Wall Street firm tries
to keep your content closed within their customer

00:20:38.169 --> 00:20:39.169
set.

00:20:39.169 --> 00:20:46.090
And this is not very loyal to my firm, but
it became very obvious to me that a sell-side

00:20:46.090 --> 00:20:52.440
analyst that was more well known was more
powerful, more impactful.

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:59.380
And so I intentionally started expanding the
distribution of this weekly pieces as far

00:20:59.380 --> 00:21:00.910
and wide as I could.

00:21:00.910 --> 00:21:03.200
Tim Ferriss: How did you expand that distribution?

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:08.000
Bill Gurley: I would leverage the sales force
and get them to give me fax numbers.

00:21:08.000 --> 00:21:10.280
That's that's where I started.

00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:17.530
I then started developing industry relationships,
which is important because you're covering

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:20.950
these companies, you start going to investor
days.

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:23.900
The buy-side is talking to these companies
as well.

00:21:23.900 --> 00:21:27.440
And so I started getting some of them on board.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:36.299
And then probably the most successful hack
of my career happened when I was invited to

00:21:36.299 --> 00:21:43.190
attend Stewart Alsop’s Agenda conference,
which I think was in Phoenix at the time.

00:21:43.190 --> 00:21:46.430
And used to be, it was the conference everyone
went to.

00:21:46.430 --> 00:21:50.240
So Gates would be in the front row, Ellison
would be there.

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:52.100
They would stay for the whole thing.

00:21:52.100 --> 00:21:53.860
You could walk up and talk to them.

00:21:53.860 --> 00:21:55.500
Michael Dell was there.

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:56.500
Everyone was there.

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:58.770
Tim Ferriss: How many analysts were invited
to such an event?

00:21:58.770 --> 00:21:59.820
Bill Gurley: I don't know.

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:05.789
Charlie had been invited and Charlie got me
in and I don't even remember.

00:22:05.789 --> 00:22:07.410
Rick Sherlund was probably there.

00:22:07.410 --> 00:22:13.490
He's a famous Microsoft analyst and it's kind
of what code is today.

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:20.130
But the difference way back then was the most
famous founder CEOs sat through the whole

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:22.539
thing and were available the whole time.

00:22:22.539 --> 00:22:26.940
Today, if one of them speaking, they come
in the back door, they go on stage, they leave.

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:29.050
They're not around.

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:30.200
So it was pretty cool.

00:22:30.200 --> 00:22:32.490
Tim Ferriss: And what was the hack at the
conference?

00:22:32.490 --> 00:22:37.549
Bill Gurley: So this was around the time where
the Palm Pilot launched.

00:22:37.549 --> 00:22:42.210
And so in the lobby of the conference was
they were selling them.

00:22:42.210 --> 00:22:45.800
They weren't free, they were selling them,
selling a Palm pilot.

00:22:45.800 --> 00:22:49.440
And I think it was like $200, $300, I can't
remember.

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:55.260
But they had put the contact information for
every attendee at the conference in the Palm

00:22:55.260 --> 00:22:56.480
Pilot.

00:22:56.480 --> 00:22:59.450
So I ran some quick math.

00:22:59.450 --> 00:23:04.169
People weren't really doing cost of customer
acquisition back then, but I think it was

00:23:04.169 --> 00:23:11.740
like 70 cents a name or something like that
of the most influential people in the entire

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.100
tech industry.

00:23:13.100 --> 00:23:19.720
And so I bought the Palm Pilot, I took it
home, and I spammed the 400 or 500 most important

00:23:19.720 --> 00:23:23.130
people in the tech industry with my weekly
newsletter.

00:23:23.130 --> 00:23:31.380
Tim Ferriss: Did you end up developing close
or closer relationships with any of them because

00:23:31.380 --> 00:23:32.380
of that newsletter?

00:23:32.380 --> 00:23:33.810
Bill Gurley: I think so.

00:23:33.810 --> 00:23:36.500
And I think also just a reputation and if
the content you produce is — I mean it's

00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:38.750
actually not that surprising today.

00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:43.420
There's plenty of content influencers all
over the place now with Substack and everything.

00:23:43.420 --> 00:23:46.780
It was just a version of that when there weren't
as many people doing it.

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:48.950
And it started with fax and became email.

00:23:48.950 --> 00:23:51.100
And today I just mostly tweeted.

00:23:51.100 --> 00:23:56.919
I don't force the distribution out of these
other things anymore and I'm not as frequent.

00:23:56.919 --> 00:24:02.670
But yeah, I mean it's a common way to build
reputation in a network and I mean you do

00:24:02.670 --> 00:24:04.640
this with your own line, so.

00:24:04.640 --> 00:24:06.850
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I do.

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:07.850
I do.

00:24:07.850 --> 00:24:14.650
So let's come back to the point you made of
helping your clients think differently, right?

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:15.650
Yeah.

00:24:15.650 --> 00:24:18.050
Presenting them with something that helps
them to think differently.

00:24:18.050 --> 00:24:23.851
And I'd like to connect that to a story I'd
love you to tell, which is about what you

00:24:23.851 --> 00:24:26.040
learned from a food analyst.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:30.169
And the question that might come before that
is how did you meet the food analyst?

00:24:30.169 --> 00:24:31.169
Why were you even having a conversation?

00:24:31.169 --> 00:24:34.870
Bill Gurley: Yeah, so when I arrived at — well,
first of all, the analysts share a floor.

00:24:34.870 --> 00:24:37.309
So we're all physically proximate.

00:24:37.309 --> 00:24:43.420
So the person that covers food or telcos or
electric utilities, we're all in the same

00:24:43.420 --> 00:24:44.440
group.

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:52.390
And the firm had efforts underway, which Charlie
Wolf ended up running to try and educate the

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:57.511
analyst in a common framework or whatever
so that we would all be better at what we

00:24:57.511 --> 00:24:59.570
do and that kind of thing.

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:07.740
And I mean, I guess birds of a feather flock
together for reasons I don't remember, me

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:12.150
and Michael J. Mauboussin, the food analyst,
started spending a lot of time together and

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:14.350
we're still close friends today.

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:20.310
He's involved at Santa Fe and I'd see him
frequently and he's done amazing things himself

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:25.669
and released several books and he's quite
well known.

00:25:25.669 --> 00:25:27.370
So we started hanging out together.

00:25:27.370 --> 00:25:35.480
He had just read a bunch of books on a framework
called Return On Invested Capital, which Stern

00:25:35.480 --> 00:25:36.960
Stewart had published on.

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:42.919
McKinsey had a book called Valuation, they
still do, that uses this type of analysis.

00:25:42.919 --> 00:25:45.181
And he was spreading it.

00:25:45.181 --> 00:25:50.840
It was the word proselytizing through the
analyst group.

00:25:50.840 --> 00:25:53.310
And I was a sponge at that point in time.

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:54.549
So I say sure.

00:25:54.549 --> 00:25:57.510
So I took the framework and ran it on all
of my companies.

00:25:57.510 --> 00:26:07.100
It turned out just by happenstance that Dell
stood out like a sore thumb with ridiculously

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:10.659
high ROIC numbers versus the rest of the industry.

00:26:10.659 --> 00:26:14.380
Like night and day, like 20 to one, wasn't
even close.

00:26:14.380 --> 00:26:19.299
And in fact, it was so ridiculous the first
time I showed him, Michael didn't believe

00:26:19.299 --> 00:26:20.380
it was true.

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:23.380
And so we got in the numbers and that kind
of thing.

00:26:23.380 --> 00:26:27.520
There's probably 10 super lucky things that
happened in my life, but him giving me that

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:33.670
framework and then at the exact same time,
Dell as a company had made two stumbles.

00:26:33.670 --> 00:26:38.880
They had an options trade that went bad and
their laptops had caught on fire.

00:26:38.880 --> 00:26:41.090
And so the stock was in the ditch.

00:26:41.090 --> 00:26:44.080
It was trading at six times earnings.

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:51.360
And we had discovered, if you will, through
this framework that they had a massive competitive

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:55.350
advantage because of this return on invested
capital thing.

00:26:55.350 --> 00:27:02.279
And so we went to a strong buy on a broken
stock and it went up 100X in the public markets

00:27:02.279 --> 00:27:03.450
from there.

00:27:03.450 --> 00:27:09.331
And I became close friends with Michael and
Tom Meredith, who was CFO at the time.

00:27:09.331 --> 00:27:10.650
They did all the work.

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:14.029
I was just along for the ride, but it was
very fortunate.

00:27:14.029 --> 00:27:20.279
Tim Ferriss: That helped put Bill Gurley on
the map in the same way that maybe eBay really

00:27:20.279 --> 00:27:23.299
thrust Benchmark into the limelight.

00:27:23.299 --> 00:27:25.100
Bill Gurley: Very much so, very much so.

00:27:25.100 --> 00:27:26.390
Especially with the buy-side community.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:28.399
Tim Ferriss: So Michael, I just wanted to
double-check this.

00:27:28.399 --> 00:27:30.090
There's a chance I've read a book of his.

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:34.710
Did he write a book called Think Twice: Harnessing
the Power of Counterintuition, or is that

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:35.740
a different Michael?

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:39.010
Bill Gurley: He's written four or five books
and he's, he's got great content.

00:27:39.010 --> 00:27:43.270
He did a Google Talk that's on YouTube that
I'd highly recommend people watch.

00:27:43.270 --> 00:27:44.530
It's just fascinating.

00:27:44.530 --> 00:27:48.909
He's been on O'Shaughnessy a couple times,
so yeah, he's worth checking out.

00:27:48.909 --> 00:27:56.860
Tim Ferriss: So in that particular case of
proselytizing, did Michael do that?

00:27:56.860 --> 00:28:05.549
Was he proselytizing and sharing this ROIC
idea because he had peers who were non-competitive

00:28:05.549 --> 00:28:11.570
and is sort of a deposit in the karmic bank
account to hopefully have some reciprocation?

00:28:11.570 --> 00:28:14.340
Or is he just a nice guy?

00:28:14.340 --> 00:28:17.370
It just seems like the environment, that would
not be true in all environments, right?

00:28:17.370 --> 00:28:18.370
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:28:18.370 --> 00:28:21.820
Tim Ferriss: That someone would take this
new insight and share it widely.

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:28.700
Bill Gurley: I think he's a natural learner,
and maybe that's what you would attribute

00:28:28.700 --> 00:28:30.360
Howard Marks' writing to.

00:28:30.360 --> 00:28:34.150
You ask people who do that, when you ask Buffett,
"Why do you write your letters?"

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:40.320
I think people believe that it helps their
mental frameworks if they write stuff down,

00:28:40.320 --> 00:28:43.130
and it challenges them.

00:28:43.130 --> 00:28:50.100
Even when I write a blog post, from the minute
I have an idea or a compulsion to say, "Hey,

00:28:50.100 --> 00:28:55.620
I'm going to write about this topic," the
process through which you actually put the

00:28:55.620 --> 00:29:00.140
words to paper and structure the paragraphs,
structure the argument, you get smarter.

00:29:00.140 --> 00:29:03.220
Sometimes you decide, "Oops, I was wrong."

00:29:03.220 --> 00:29:05.769
You learn by putting it all together.

00:29:05.769 --> 00:29:08.190
And Michael's always been that way.

00:29:08.190 --> 00:29:16.740
He's pretty much built a career out of being
someone who studies companies, valuation frameworks,

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:18.710
how investors win.

00:29:18.710 --> 00:29:25.730
I mean, he's gone deep on things like Myers-Briggs
on different investors and structural, how

00:29:25.730 --> 00:29:30.010
do you organize an investment team to be most
— all kinds of stuff like that.

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:34.190
And it's what fascinates him, and he's a synthesizer.

00:29:34.190 --> 00:29:38.690
And I think some of the best non-fiction writers
are synthesizers.

00:29:38.690 --> 00:29:44.230
People say, "Oh, Michael Porter, Competitive
Strategy," 14 other people had written that

00:29:44.230 --> 00:29:45.410
stuff before.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:49.049
Well, he wrote it in a really compact way
that's easy to read.

00:29:49.049 --> 00:29:50.240
That's super helpful.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:55.870
And Michael does that with — he goes out
and reads the stuff that's super hard to read,

00:29:55.870 --> 00:30:00.140
that's overly academic or whatnot, and packages
in a way that people can consume.

00:30:00.140 --> 00:30:01.400
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:30:01.400 --> 00:30:07.419
I remember Kevin Kelly said to me once, and
I'm paraphrasing, but I really enjoy Kevin

00:30:07.419 --> 00:30:08.419
Kelly.

00:30:08.419 --> 00:30:14.500
For people who don't know, look him up, KK.org,
and he was saying, "I don't write to express

00:30:14.500 --> 00:30:15.659
what I think.

00:30:15.659 --> 00:30:20.990
I write in order to think or discover what
I think or clarify what I think."

00:30:20.990 --> 00:30:25.909
So the taking of the ROIC from, in this case,
let's just say food and applying it to Dell,

00:30:25.909 --> 00:30:30.260
was this sort of translational move.

00:30:30.260 --> 00:30:36.179
So I want to read something from, I think
this was a Vox interview, and you can't believe

00:30:36.179 --> 00:30:38.590
everything you read on the internet, but it
would make sense.

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:40.250
So this is related to OpenTable.

00:30:40.250 --> 00:30:43.861
So, "Having come out of OpenTable being successful,"
this is quoting you, "I was trying to think

00:30:43.861 --> 00:30:48.861
of other industries where, if you put a network
on top of [it, that] would absorb waste and

00:30:48.861 --> 00:30:50.799
make it more efficient and more usable."

00:30:50.799 --> 00:30:58.640
And this is within the context of looking
for something that would basically appear

00:30:58.640 --> 00:30:59.640
like Uber.

00:30:59.640 --> 00:31:03.309
So not working within the taxi framework but
with black cars.

00:31:03.309 --> 00:31:10.019
And I'm just curious where else you've applied
that type of translation where you see a case

00:31:10.019 --> 00:31:15.909
study or a successful proof of a network being
laid on top of something and then applying

00:31:15.909 --> 00:31:17.010
it to something else.

00:31:17.010 --> 00:31:19.650
Are there other examples of translating in
that way?

00:31:19.650 --> 00:31:20.650
Bill Gurley: Absolutely.

00:31:20.650 --> 00:31:21.650
And by the way, OpenTable was built on top.

00:31:21.650 --> 00:31:24.659
Our impetus to do OpenTable was based upon
something like that.

00:31:24.659 --> 00:31:32.309
So in 1996, Brian Arthur, who was at the Santa
Fe Institute back then, published an article

00:31:32.309 --> 00:31:38.570
in Harvard Business Review called “Increasing
Returns and the Two Worlds of Business.”

00:31:38.570 --> 00:31:42.190
And it was really the first piece that talked
about network effects.

00:31:42.190 --> 00:31:47.070
Of course, Microsoft was already starting
to really take off, but this idea of network

00:31:47.070 --> 00:31:51.929
effects is that some industries are going
to be structured where you get win or take

00:31:51.929 --> 00:31:56.049
most, and the more successful you are, you
get locked in.

00:31:56.049 --> 00:32:00.120
And Brian talked about things just like the
Microsoft UIs.

00:32:00.120 --> 00:32:04.100
You know how Word works, you get comfortable
with how it works, and then switching has

00:32:04.100 --> 00:32:08.909
cost and those kind of things, and sharing
documents and collaboration.

00:32:08.909 --> 00:32:12.570
And Zoom obviously has massive network effects.

00:32:12.570 --> 00:32:20.889
So we started looking for those things because
they tend to cause outlier outcomes.

00:32:20.889 --> 00:32:25.950
So I can remember, and this gets into back
to your rule stuff, when I met with Chuck

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:29.929
Templeton, the founder of OpenTable, and he
had three restaurants.

00:32:29.929 --> 00:32:36.370
You had to believe a lot to get from that
point to the global phenomenon that it became.

00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:40.981
And the bet that we talked about making when
we said, "Okay, let's go do this thing," is

00:32:40.981 --> 00:32:48.770
"If we get enough restaurants on this thing,
then the consumers will come.

00:32:48.770 --> 00:32:51.600
And if the consumers come, then people will
have to get on."

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:52.900
And that's what happened.

00:32:52.900 --> 00:32:58.970
But you had to believe it because otherwise,
at the time, we were selling PCs to restaurant

00:32:58.970 --> 00:33:00.889
owners, and guess what?

00:33:00.889 --> 00:33:03.690
They didn't have connectivity at the time.

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:09.289
So we had to partner with someone to get broadband
installed, which wasn't easy.

00:33:09.289 --> 00:33:11.490
I mean, it was all bad.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:17.279
So your normal work set, we're putting a piece
of hardware in a small to medium business

00:33:17.279 --> 00:33:22.160
that didn't have a lot of money, and we had
to provision broadband.

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:25.020
So you would normally go, "Don't do that."

00:33:25.020 --> 00:33:30.539
But I remember my favorite proof point of
that network effect, and then I'll get to

00:33:30.539 --> 00:33:32.200
some of the other models.

00:33:32.200 --> 00:33:40.390
At one point we were reviewing the sales force
productivity, and our model when we scaled

00:33:40.390 --> 00:33:45.159
up the business that we had or each salesperson
had to close four restaurants a month.

00:33:45.159 --> 00:33:49.400
And at the time we were up to 7.7, so we were
feeling pretty good.

00:33:49.400 --> 00:33:51.760
Things were starting to break our way.

00:33:51.760 --> 00:33:57.370
And in the board meeting, they laid out the
list of all the salespeople, and one of them

00:33:57.370 --> 00:34:01.179
had done 35, 35 restaurants in a month.

00:34:01.179 --> 00:34:04.279
And so I asked the question, "Who is that
salesperson?"

00:34:04.279 --> 00:34:10.560
And OpenTable started in San Francisco, and
we played local game.

00:34:10.560 --> 00:34:12.020
We didn't go everywhere at once.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:14.030
We built liquidity city by city.

00:34:14.030 --> 00:34:19.200
Anyway, at the time, that salesperson was
the one salesperson left in San Francisco

00:34:19.200 --> 00:34:22.060
where we had 90 percent penetration.

00:34:22.060 --> 00:34:26.139
And so the 35 were coming out of that last
10.

00:34:26.139 --> 00:34:29.010
And that individual was basically taking orders.

00:34:29.010 --> 00:34:36.540
And that to me was like, "Oh, yeah, the network
effects really working here."

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:38.669
So anyway, we look for that.

00:34:38.669 --> 00:34:40.020
Some other frameworks, SaaS.

00:34:40.020 --> 00:34:43.149
Tim Ferriss: Could I actually pause for one
second before you get to SaaS?

00:34:43.149 --> 00:34:47.419
So you talked about you have to believe, you
mentioned you have to believe a lot of things

00:34:47.419 --> 00:34:53.470
can go right in order to invest in a business
like OpenTable at the time.

00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:58.040
So now I would love to know what contributed
to your ability to have conviction?

00:34:58.040 --> 00:35:04.350
I mean, how much of it was potential network
effects versus total addressable market versus

00:35:04.350 --> 00:35:05.930
founder versus other stuff?

00:35:05.930 --> 00:35:10.580
Bill Gurley: Oh, I mean, I would say in some
of these verticals, both OpenTable and Uber,

00:35:10.580 --> 00:35:14.369
there were years and years and years where
everyone thought the TAM was too small.

00:35:14.369 --> 00:35:16.890
In fact, oh, here's a great story.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:23.150
So this was in '99, so this is a 23-year-old
story, but I had successfully recruited a

00:35:23.150 --> 00:35:30.710
CFO from a public company, which you could
do back in those glory days to come into OpenTable.

00:35:30.710 --> 00:35:37.520
And one day I showed up early for a board
meeting, and the CFO comes to me and he says,

00:35:37.520 --> 00:35:38.520
"Bill, I'm going to quit."

00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:39.810
And I said, "Okay."

00:35:39.810 --> 00:35:42.180
I go, "Why are you going to quit?"

00:35:42.180 --> 00:35:44.740
And he goes, "This business will never work."

00:35:44.740 --> 00:35:47.300
And I said, "Okay, why will it never work?"

00:35:47.300 --> 00:35:49.520
And he says, "Well, my model says it'll never
work."

00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:51.310
So I said, "Show me your model."

00:35:51.310 --> 00:35:58.950
So we look at the model and I dive in, and
he has frozen penetration in each city at

00:35:58.950 --> 00:36:00.470
17 percent.

00:36:00.470 --> 00:36:02.339
And he had come from a retail business.

00:36:02.339 --> 00:36:05.230
And I go, "Why'd you freeze it at 17 percent?"

00:36:05.230 --> 00:36:10.060
And he said, "Oh, no one gets more than 17
percent market share, all the businesses I've

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:11.140
worked with."

00:36:11.140 --> 00:36:16.020
And because I believed in network effects,
I was like, "We're going to get 99.

00:36:16.020 --> 00:36:19.300
We're not stopping at 17, we're going to get
99 percent.

00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:22.080
You don't understand how this is going to
work."

00:36:22.080 --> 00:36:26.130
And that's because I believed in network effects
and he didn't.

00:36:26.130 --> 00:36:28.891
When we filed the S-1, I really wanted to
FedEx it.

00:36:28.891 --> 00:36:30.500
Tim Ferriss: Did he stay or did he leave?

00:36:30.500 --> 00:36:31.830
Bill Gurley: No, he left.

00:36:31.830 --> 00:36:34.700
He quit, and I decided not to FedEx in the
S-1.

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:39.960
I figured he had seen, but by then we were
kicking off massive cash flow.

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:40.960
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

00:36:40.960 --> 00:36:41.960
Bill Gurley: It's cool.

00:36:41.960 --> 00:36:42.960
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:36:42.960 --> 00:36:45.400
So you're talking about models, and I interrupted
you.

00:36:45.400 --> 00:36:46.579
You were going to jump to SaaS.

00:36:46.579 --> 00:36:48.780
Bill Gurley: Yeah, I was just going to mention
a bunch of them.

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:49.780
I mean —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, please.

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:50.780
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:57.560
So I did my first SaaS deal in '99, and that
category of companies is still bearing fruit.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:02.890
People have made tons of money, and now it's
common but at the time it wasn't.

00:37:02.890 --> 00:37:09.450
And you transformed an entire industry, open
source, Benchmark probably had, I don't know,

00:37:09.450 --> 00:37:15.790
eight or nine successful open source companies
where you've used that model to attack an

00:37:15.790 --> 00:37:21.480
industry that's preexisting, but where someone
has proprietary technology.

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:26.599
Social networks, I mean, we were fortunate
to be in Twitter, Snapchat, Instagram, and

00:37:26.599 --> 00:37:31.220
Matt Cohler, who was a partner at Benchmark,
developed kind of a sixth sense on what a

00:37:31.220 --> 00:37:33.480
breakout social network looks like.

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:40.290
And we've had a few that missed too, but the
outsized nature of the winds are so high.

00:37:40.290 --> 00:37:46.250
So there's like four or five different ones
that we look for, network effects, open source,

00:37:46.250 --> 00:37:47.420
and you get good at it.

00:37:47.420 --> 00:37:53.079
You start to understand what works, what doesn't,
what leads to success, what doesn't.

00:37:53.079 --> 00:37:54.859
I mean, there's nuances.

00:37:54.859 --> 00:38:00.480
In open source, with that business model,
which is you're basically packaging support

00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:06.160
and reliability, because someone could just
download it for free so how do you charge?

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:11.470
And this goes back to Michael Porter, but
if you have a very consolidated industry of

00:38:11.470 --> 00:38:13.900
big companies, they don't pay.

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:17.950
They just hire the people that know how to
use the product.

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:20.030
MySQL is an example.

00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:23.340
Google and Yahoo were two of the biggest customers,
never paid us a penny.

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:29.440
You need that product to go into the corporate
world where people want that kind of handholding,

00:38:29.440 --> 00:38:31.000
and that's where you get paid.

00:38:31.000 --> 00:38:36.630
So you can't just be, "Oh, I love open source,"
because then you get into something where

00:38:36.630 --> 00:38:41.430
it's serving a much more finite set of customers,
and then you can't make the model work.

00:38:41.430 --> 00:38:44.890
So you have to learn the esoteric nuances.

00:38:44.890 --> 00:38:49.740
But anyway, yeah, there are many veins and
venture that get mined over and over and over

00:38:49.740 --> 00:38:51.589
again if they're big enough.

00:38:51.589 --> 00:38:57.440
Tim Ferriss: And this is maybe a novice question,
but I'm curious, what rules did you guys have

00:38:57.440 --> 00:39:00.099
around bet sizing?

00:39:00.099 --> 00:39:02.970
To use maybe a poker analogy.

00:39:02.970 --> 00:39:08.440
How do you think about the parameters around
the size of the check that you cut?

00:39:08.440 --> 00:39:11.780
Just in terms of long-term strategy.

00:39:11.780 --> 00:39:15.880
Bill Gurley: Benchmark's a very particular
firm, and we've stayed — we've wandered

00:39:15.880 --> 00:39:20.240
off course a couple times, but we've always
come back to our kind of home base.

00:39:20.240 --> 00:39:26.810
And we've remained committed and focused to
very early stage investing, and we're pretty

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:31.000
much market takers on what that first check
size is.

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:32.540
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by that?

00:39:32.540 --> 00:39:37.130
Bill Gurley: I mean, there's a competitive
dynamic in the industry that kind of defines

00:39:37.130 --> 00:39:42.760
what a series A check looks like or sometimes
series B. If we thought something was going

00:39:42.760 --> 00:39:47.650
to be an outsized winner, we wouldn't miss
it because market was saying that had to be

00:39:47.650 --> 00:39:49.790
15 or five.

00:39:49.790 --> 00:39:50.790
It's more like a ticket.

00:39:50.790 --> 00:39:53.540
Like refining —
Tim Ferriss: 15 or five, you're talking about

00:39:53.540 --> 00:39:54.900
15 or five million, right?

00:39:54.900 --> 00:39:55.900
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:39:55.900 --> 00:39:59.710
Tim Ferriss: So would the guiding principle
in a case that be the percentage of ownership

00:39:59.710 --> 00:40:01.170
that you would end up with?

00:40:01.170 --> 00:40:02.170
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:40:02.170 --> 00:40:05.110
Much more, something we care way more about
than the size of the check.

00:40:05.110 --> 00:40:06.110
Yeah, yeah.

00:40:06.110 --> 00:40:07.110
And we're not the only one.

00:40:07.110 --> 00:40:08.550
A lot of venture firms operate that way.

00:40:08.550 --> 00:40:09.550
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, sure.

00:40:09.550 --> 00:40:10.550
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:40:10.550 --> 00:40:17.010
So board seat is the constraint, and then
ownership is the optimization variable, whatever

00:40:17.010 --> 00:40:21.069
the math is that follows out of that is what
you take.

00:40:21.069 --> 00:40:26.560
Tim Ferriss: So to be right and contrarian,
I mean, I guess sometimes in venture, I'm

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:33.589
not sure if it — I mean, I'm curious how
you cultivate that in partner meetings.

00:40:33.589 --> 00:40:37.330
What is the format of a successful partner
meeting when someone's getting up and saying,

00:40:37.330 --> 00:40:38.330
"Here's my deal."

00:40:38.330 --> 00:40:44.980
Bill Gurley: Well, one thing I would offer
as a preamble to at least our process is the

00:40:44.980 --> 00:40:51.099
founding partners of Benchmark did something
that hadn't been done before in venture, which

00:40:51.099 --> 00:40:53.290
is they created an equal partnership.

00:40:53.290 --> 00:41:00.339
And most business partnerships, and this includes
private equity and law firms and real estate

00:41:00.339 --> 00:41:02.240
firms, there's a hierarchy.

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:06.930
And the people that have been there the longest
sit at the very top, and they take an outsized

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:08.230
amount.

00:41:08.230 --> 00:41:13.130
And some of our founding partners had worked
at those firms and felt like the young people

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:19.579
did more of the work, and therefore it wasn't
conducive to the right type of internal behavior.

00:41:19.579 --> 00:41:23.220
And so they created this notion of an equal
partnership.

00:41:23.220 --> 00:41:28.170
And even today when we go hire a new partner,
they come in and they have an equal seat at

00:41:28.170 --> 00:41:29.170
the table.

00:41:29.170 --> 00:41:32.359
We just divide the pie, which is very different.

00:41:32.359 --> 00:41:37.900
The reason I think that's important to any
discussion about our own processes is I think

00:41:37.900 --> 00:41:44.700
that has a number of dynamics that kind of
emerge from being equal.

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:49.500
I think everyone's voice is heard, whereas
if you had a hierarchical firm and big boss

00:41:49.500 --> 00:41:54.240
walked in and said, "Blah, blah, blah," that
was carry still much more weight, right?

00:41:54.240 --> 00:41:55.240
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:59.520
Bill Gurley: And so you feel like your voice
is heard when you come in.

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:01.160
I think we root for one another.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:06.700
So if I'm at a hierarchical firm that's up
or out, I'm kind of competing with my peers

00:42:06.700 --> 00:42:08.579
here because we're going to divide it equally.

00:42:08.579 --> 00:42:13.520
I want our new partners to be as successful
as they possibly can, and I felt that on the

00:42:13.520 --> 00:42:17.579
way in, and I feel the desire to do it on
the way out.

00:42:17.579 --> 00:42:19.750
And those are just natural emergent properties.

00:42:19.750 --> 00:42:24.490
And the answer, so then to come around to
your question, how do you be contrarian?

00:42:24.490 --> 00:42:29.760
How do you have — well, I mentioned we have
this phrase, "What could go right?"

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:36.730
You're just constantly thinking devil's advocate
in your mind and devil's advocate on the positive

00:42:36.730 --> 00:42:40.190
side, like what would it take for this thing
to break out?

00:42:40.190 --> 00:42:41.880
Are we making a mistake?

00:42:41.880 --> 00:42:44.849
Could you imagine this being really, really
big?

00:42:44.849 --> 00:42:48.120
And what would have to happen for that to
happen.

00:42:48.120 --> 00:42:50.650
When someone new comes along, you'd be surprised.

00:42:50.650 --> 00:42:57.059
I have specific memories of new partners coming
in, and we'll just tell them, "Bring all the

00:42:57.059 --> 00:42:58.130
companies you meet in.

00:42:58.130 --> 00:42:59.480
Just bring them in."

00:42:59.480 --> 00:43:04.340
And those companies will present, and then
we talk about them afterwards, and that's

00:43:04.340 --> 00:43:05.340
—
Tim Ferriss: Into the partner meeting?

00:43:05.340 --> 00:43:06.340
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:43:06.340 --> 00:43:10.770
And that's where the learning process gets
passed down.

00:43:10.770 --> 00:43:16.920
I'm sure just like storytelling in a tribe
300 years ago or 400 years ago, right?

00:43:16.920 --> 00:43:19.140
That is that dynamic.

00:43:19.140 --> 00:43:21.820
And it's weird.

00:43:21.820 --> 00:43:28.069
A lot of people say venture is at the end
of the day, a pattern recognition job, but

00:43:28.069 --> 00:43:30.450
we also talked about those rules can get you
in trouble.

00:43:30.450 --> 00:43:36.470
So you're trying to create these kind of loose
pattern recognition so that you can be helpful

00:43:36.470 --> 00:43:40.010
and identify things, and then you want to
pass those along.

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:46.070
And then you got to constantly check it because
of what we said about how you could miss something.

00:43:46.070 --> 00:43:50.001
But when that new partner comes in and those
are happening, I can remember, and I'll leave

00:43:50.001 --> 00:43:55.490
the names out, but one of the new partners,
he brought in company one, "No," company two,

00:43:55.490 --> 00:43:56.490
"No."

00:43:56.490 --> 00:43:57.710
He was very frustrated.

00:43:57.710 --> 00:44:01.990
And then company 10 he brought in, and we
were like, "Try and close that immediately,"

00:44:01.990 --> 00:44:04.380
just the complete opposite.

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:06.090
And he remembers that too.

00:44:06.090 --> 00:44:14.380
But you're starting to learn, you're starting
to pick up the collective wisdom of the group.

00:44:14.380 --> 00:44:20.140
And it is a bit of an art form because in
that window between when you meet a company

00:44:20.140 --> 00:44:27.750
and when you might try and close the investment,
I bet you the average is under 60 days.

00:44:27.750 --> 00:44:29.480
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:30.480
For sure.

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:32.100
Bill Gurley: It might even be closer to three
weeks.

00:44:32.100 --> 00:44:35.690
And so it all happened super fast.

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:41.040
Tim Ferriss: Was there any feedback that you
recall or could just hypothetically imagine

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:47.940
you gave that new partner that helped him
or her to hone in on the 10th that was then

00:44:47.940 --> 00:44:49.520
a "Yes, close this immediately?"

00:44:49.520 --> 00:44:50.520
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:44:50.520 --> 00:44:55.150
But I mean, a lot of it goes back to what
you might pick up in competitive strategy.

00:44:55.150 --> 00:44:59.620
The two books that I would put for the venture
community, for the startup community that

00:44:59.620 --> 00:45:06.200
I would put as next level right on top of
Competitive Strategy or Innovator's Dilemma,

00:45:06.200 --> 00:45:11.700
which does an amazing job of describing why
startups can compete with big companies.

00:45:11.700 --> 00:45:12.700
Amazing.

00:45:12.700 --> 00:45:20.320
And Crossing the Chasm, which does a really
good job of explaining how a startup should

00:45:20.320 --> 00:45:24.599
kind of sequence their customer base as they
grow.

00:45:24.599 --> 00:45:27.050
And they're both fundamental.

00:45:27.050 --> 00:45:31.960
And you just pick up a number of these different
things, and I'll give you an example because

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:33.339
you start to develop intuition.

00:45:33.339 --> 00:45:37.790
So a company where we had a quick yes on that
I'm on the board of is called HackerOne.

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:44.000
So at the time of HackerOne's founding, there
were four companies that used white hat hackers

00:45:44.000 --> 00:45:45.830
to make their sites more secure.

00:45:45.830 --> 00:45:49.810
Microsoft, Google, Facebook, and Mozilla,
they were the only four.

00:45:49.810 --> 00:45:51.460
No one else did it.

00:45:51.460 --> 00:45:57.110
And yet people would say, I remember seeing
an interview with Sheryl Sandberg where she

00:45:57.110 --> 00:46:01.099
said, someone asked her about privacy and
security, and she says, "Well, the best thing

00:46:01.099 --> 00:46:05.060
we have is this bug bounty program."

00:46:05.060 --> 00:46:07.410
And she talks about it for a while and the
crowd applauds.

00:46:07.410 --> 00:46:12.760
And so one of the individuals that worked
on the Facebook program came up with a business

00:46:12.760 --> 00:46:17.990
idea of, "Why don't we build this for the
other companies in the universe, like every

00:46:17.990 --> 00:46:19.630
other company in the universe?"

00:46:19.630 --> 00:46:25.050
And when they presented, we didn't even discuss
the company.

00:46:25.050 --> 00:46:28.900
The minute they left, we were like, "Okay,
how are we going to close this?"

00:46:28.900 --> 00:46:33.859
Everyone had just jumped to "Yes," because
it seemed so tautological, that there's no

00:46:33.859 --> 00:46:37.430
way this thing's great for these four companies
and no one else.

00:46:37.430 --> 00:46:43.230
And it turned out that those four companies
had a group of 20 or 30 people running the

00:46:43.230 --> 00:46:45.040
program, which other companies couldn't do.

00:46:45.040 --> 00:46:50.430
So it was just natural that if you ran that
as a service, people would jump in.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:54.290
That's one that we got too fast.

00:46:54.290 --> 00:46:59.480
Tim Ferriss: So you've mentioned a number
of your partners, Matt Cohler, who was also

00:46:59.480 --> 00:47:06.160
very helpful for Uber, seems to be very good
at using himself as the guinea pig.

00:47:06.160 --> 00:47:07.160
Very smart guy.

00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:12.559
What are some of the lessons you've learned
from your partners?

00:47:12.559 --> 00:47:15.940
And that could be anyone, Fenton, Lasky, Cohler,
anyone.

00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:17.619
You don't even necessarily have to mention
names.

00:47:17.619 --> 00:47:24.990
I'm just curious what their superpowers are
or what you have adapted, learned from them.

00:47:24.990 --> 00:47:28.540
Bill Gurley: So I'd probably go back to some
of the founding partners because those were

00:47:28.540 --> 00:47:29.869
the ones that I learned from.

00:47:29.869 --> 00:47:30.869
Tim Ferriss: Let's do it.

00:47:30.869 --> 00:47:35.270
Bill Gurley: One of them, I'd say from Bruce
Dunlevie, is just have a really big tent.

00:47:35.270 --> 00:47:40.060
We're in a networking business where you're
trying to look under every rock for the next

00:47:40.060 --> 00:47:41.290
possible deal.

00:47:41.290 --> 00:47:47.490
And cutting off avenues of information or
flow is just a really stupid idea.

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:52.319
And so it ties in with what could go right,
but just be super open-minded.

00:47:52.319 --> 00:47:58.550
And so Bruce had been involved with a company
that I think was in a software tool space,

00:47:58.550 --> 00:47:59.550
the name escapes me.

00:47:59.550 --> 00:48:01.740
We could look it up.

00:48:01.740 --> 00:48:09.290
And had developed a lot of friendships, went
down the organizational chart at the company.

00:48:09.290 --> 00:48:13.810
And one day, one of the engineers from that
company comes to him and says, "I'm working

00:48:13.810 --> 00:48:17.900
on this marketplace thing called eBay."

00:48:17.900 --> 00:48:22.559
And he was from a different industry, Bruce,
but because he had developed that relationship

00:48:22.559 --> 00:48:28.450
that came in, now, it turned out that Bob
Kagle was much more excited about eBay than

00:48:28.450 --> 00:48:29.630
Bruce was.

00:48:29.630 --> 00:48:34.700
And so Bruce made that intro because we're
equal partnership, that's all cool.

00:48:34.700 --> 00:48:40.050
And Bob ends up doing the deal and becomes
the number-one-ranked VC in the land.

00:48:40.050 --> 00:48:42.140
But it all came from that relationship.

00:48:42.140 --> 00:48:43.589
So big tent.

00:48:43.589 --> 00:48:50.480
The older I get, I just take the time, be
kind, be available, it pays off.

00:48:50.480 --> 00:48:52.980
At least in our industry, it pays off big
time.

00:48:52.980 --> 00:48:54.359
So that's one.

00:48:54.359 --> 00:48:59.069
Venture, another one is just, and this is
more specific to venture, but unless you're

00:48:59.069 --> 00:49:06.370
super lucky — there were two VCs I know
that were very fortunate, Cohler and Roelof,

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:12.240
both at Sequoia who — Roelof with YouTube
and Matt with Instagram, where they had a

00:49:12.240 --> 00:49:14.559
hit two years in.

00:49:14.559 --> 00:49:20.060
But for the vast majority of venture capitalists,
they don't have a liquidity event till year

00:49:20.060 --> 00:49:21.680
eight or nine.

00:49:21.680 --> 00:49:25.430
And so it's easy to doubt yourself.

00:49:25.430 --> 00:49:31.859
And three years in, people like to use the
child age analogy, you've got 12-year-olds

00:49:31.859 --> 00:49:37.530
becoming 13-year-olds, and your whole portfolio's
like that with acne all over their face, and

00:49:37.530 --> 00:49:40.030
you can really lose confidence.

00:49:40.030 --> 00:49:45.910
So one thing that a lot of my partners did
was just like, "It's going to be okay, get

00:49:45.910 --> 00:49:46.920
back out there.

00:49:46.920 --> 00:49:47.920
You're good.

00:49:47.920 --> 00:49:49.200
You're doing fine."

00:49:49.200 --> 00:49:54.280
That kind of thing, which was way more helpful
than you could possibly imagine because the

00:49:54.280 --> 00:49:56.000
anxiety was spiking.

00:49:56.000 --> 00:50:01.650
Tim Ferriss: So I want to come back to open
source in a minute, but first I'm wondering,

00:50:01.650 --> 00:50:06.940
you've mentioned a number of books that are
broadly applicable to entrepreneurship, and

00:50:06.940 --> 00:50:11.640
I'm sure translate to, as you mentioned, investing
in a number of capacities.

00:50:11.640 --> 00:50:20.160
If you were advising someone or just mentoring
someone who wanted to learn how to be a good

00:50:20.160 --> 00:50:26.500
angel investor or venture capitalist in tech,
what might you suggest to them in terms of

00:50:26.500 --> 00:50:28.589
approach or resources, books, anything?

00:50:28.589 --> 00:50:32.560
Bill Gurley: I'll give you a few more books
and then obviously the world's different today,

00:50:32.560 --> 00:50:35.200
the content consumption is so different.

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:39.210
But there's a book I love called Startup by
Kaplan.

00:50:39.210 --> 00:50:40.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:45.660
I bought that when I first moved to Silicon
Valley in '99.

00:50:45.660 --> 00:50:47.589
That was one of the very first books.

00:50:47.589 --> 00:50:49.950
Bill Gurley: Yeah, it's just fantastic.

00:50:49.950 --> 00:50:54.290
He was in the — what do they call that world?

00:50:54.290 --> 00:50:55.751
Because it's where General Magic was.

00:50:55.751 --> 00:50:58.790
It was like the first portable computers.

00:50:58.790 --> 00:51:05.250
Every one of them failed, every one, but they
all raised massive amounts of money.

00:51:05.250 --> 00:51:12.570
He, on his ride home every day, had a cassette
tape and left an archive or a diary, which

00:51:12.570 --> 00:51:16.740
makes the book so good because the detail
is fantastic.

00:51:16.740 --> 00:51:21.700
But they had the best venture capitalists,
they had the best advisors, they had the best

00:51:21.700 --> 00:51:27.540
executives, and the executives that were in
— this was GO Corp, I think, that were there.

00:51:27.540 --> 00:51:29.670
All went on to do amazing things.

00:51:29.670 --> 00:51:33.900
But this company failed hard, failed hard.

00:51:33.900 --> 00:51:40.110
And I think it's nice to combine a book of
failure with all the books of success.

00:51:40.110 --> 00:51:42.780
I just think it's super educational.

00:51:42.780 --> 00:51:47.770
Interestingly, if you read that, you should
probably also simultaneously watch the General

00:51:47.770 --> 00:51:48.770
Magic documentary.

00:51:48.770 --> 00:51:50.170
Tim Ferriss: It's so good.

00:51:50.170 --> 00:51:51.170
Yeah.

00:51:51.170 --> 00:51:54.690
Bill Gurley: Because they were a competitor
with GO, so it'd be — and the same story.

00:51:54.690 --> 00:51:58.690
And the people there all went on to wild success.

00:51:58.690 --> 00:52:00.369
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:52:00.369 --> 00:52:07.750
It's like the Jodorowsky's Dune where Giger,
who went on to create the iconic design for

00:52:07.750 --> 00:52:08.859
Alien and Aliens.

00:52:08.859 --> 00:52:16.069
I mean, they're just these failures that contain
an all-star team and fail for any number of

00:52:16.069 --> 00:52:18.589
factors, but General Magic, I just want to
second that recommendation.

00:52:18.589 --> 00:52:19.589
Bill Gurley: Yeah, it's fantastic.

00:52:19.589 --> 00:52:24.069
Tim Ferriss: If people want an additional
inside look, listen to my interview with Tony

00:52:24.069 --> 00:52:26.540
Fadell, which goes into great depth on that.

00:52:26.540 --> 00:52:29.250
Bill Gurley: And on Tony, I'd read his new
book, Build.

00:52:29.250 --> 00:52:30.260
Tim Ferriss: It's solid.

00:52:30.260 --> 00:52:31.260
It's very strong.

00:52:31.260 --> 00:52:32.260
Yeah.

00:52:32.260 --> 00:52:35.010
Bill Gurley: And it's got a lot of frameworks,
and you can agree with them or not agree with

00:52:35.010 --> 00:52:36.010
them.

00:52:36.010 --> 00:52:39.900
But I do think entrepreneurs benefit from
frameworks because just running by the seat

00:52:39.900 --> 00:52:44.650
of your pants, you need a framework for having
weekly meetings.

00:52:44.650 --> 00:52:48.560
You need a framework for executive recruiting.

00:52:48.560 --> 00:52:52.720
You need a framework for development of your
team.

00:52:52.720 --> 00:52:54.500
And there are a lot of good ones.

00:52:54.500 --> 00:52:58.940
There's probably 20 good ones of each of those,
but the worst thing you can possibly do is

00:52:58.940 --> 00:53:02.140
have no framework and just kind of seat of
your pants.

00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:08.780
And by the way, that's the most frequent solution,
the absolute no framework.

00:53:08.780 --> 00:53:10.680
I would mention Shoe Dog.

00:53:10.680 --> 00:53:17.720
I don't know if the reality had as many near-death
experiences as the book makes it sound like,

00:53:17.720 --> 00:53:21.920
but it's good to see the tenacity you need.

00:53:21.920 --> 00:53:28.619
This is the Phil Knight Nike book, but the
tenacity you need to make it is high.

00:53:28.619 --> 00:53:32.210
You shouldn't start — if you're starting
a company because you think it's going to

00:53:32.210 --> 00:53:34.119
be a good lifestyle, holy shit.

00:53:34.119 --> 00:53:36.150
You're in for a rude awakening.

00:53:36.150 --> 00:53:37.150
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

00:53:37.150 --> 00:53:43.620
So the same writer who penned Shoe Dog in
reality is the same person who did Open, the

00:53:43.620 --> 00:53:47.470
autobiography of Agassi, which is another
spectacular book.

00:53:47.470 --> 00:53:49.480
Bill Gurley: And apparently the new Harry
book that I —

00:53:49.480 --> 00:53:51.280
Tim Ferriss: Oh, no kidding.

00:53:51.280 --> 00:53:52.280
All right.

00:53:52.280 --> 00:53:55.950
That's a selling point because I think it's
Moehringer is a spectacular, spectacular —

00:53:55.950 --> 00:53:56.950
Bill Gurley: J.D.

00:53:56.950 --> 00:53:57.950
Moehringer, yeah, yeah.

00:53:57.950 --> 00:53:58.950
He's fantastic.

00:53:58.950 --> 00:53:59.950
Tim Ferriss: Okay.

00:53:59.950 --> 00:54:00.950
So Shoe Dog.

00:54:00.950 --> 00:54:04.360
So far, these are company-building books,
which is totally valid, of course.

00:54:04.360 --> 00:54:08.540
Bill Gurley: For kind of high-tech macro,
if there is such a thing, I think Ridley's

00:54:08.540 --> 00:54:13.710
two books, The Rational Optimist and How Innovation
Works, they're just fantastic and spectacular.

00:54:13.710 --> 00:54:17.310
They're much higher level, but they're really,
really good.

00:54:17.310 --> 00:54:18.840
Tim Ferriss: What makes them good?

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:23.980
Bill Gurley: Well, the first book, Ridley
has this point of view that I think is very

00:54:23.980 --> 00:54:29.640
hard to dispute, that the vast majority of
wealth creation and increase of standard of

00:54:29.640 --> 00:54:35.050
living for humans on the planet come from
two things, commerce and ideas.

00:54:35.050 --> 00:54:37.400
What he calls "ideas having sex."

00:54:37.400 --> 00:54:40.890
So if you think about if someone comes up
with a new farming technique, the marginal

00:54:40.890 --> 00:54:42.630
cost of that is zero.

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:46.690
And if you pass it along to someone, their
productivity improves.

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:51.900
And so it's super powerful in my mind, especially
when I think about people.

00:54:51.900 --> 00:54:56.119
A lot of people on the planet want to improve
the standard of living of a lot of people.

00:54:56.119 --> 00:55:04.420
And I look at what Deng Xiaoping did in China
and say, I don't know that any other human

00:55:04.420 --> 00:55:10.440
in the history of the world has unlocked as
much standard of living increase as this one

00:55:10.440 --> 00:55:13.710
human by bringing capitalism to China.

00:55:13.710 --> 00:55:17.700
And anyway, it's something that really speaks
to me in terms —

00:55:17.700 --> 00:55:23.450
Tim Ferriss: On a much smaller scale, but
people should study Lee Kuan Yew and well,

00:55:23.450 --> 00:55:24.450
just the whole Singapore story.

00:55:24.450 --> 00:55:29.839
I mean, basically taking it from a swamp to
what it is today is just an incredible, but

00:55:29.839 --> 00:55:31.400
I agree on the Deng Xiaoping piece.

00:55:31.400 --> 00:55:32.400
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:55:32.400 --> 00:55:36.020
So then and on other content, the world's
way different now, right?

00:55:36.020 --> 00:55:42.910
With podcasts and blogs and Twitter and all
these kind of things so no matter what you're

00:55:42.910 --> 00:55:48.099
doing, but especially if you want to do venture
attack, there's lots of people that talk their

00:55:48.099 --> 00:55:53.990
book, that talk their game like follow, listen,
read, consume, consume, read, read, read,

00:55:53.990 --> 00:55:54.990
read.

00:55:54.990 --> 00:55:56.510
I don't think you can get too much information.

00:55:56.510 --> 00:55:59.230
Tim Ferriss: Well, let's segue to open source.

00:55:59.230 --> 00:56:02.369
What else do you think open source might be
able to solve?

00:56:02.369 --> 00:56:08.460
And I've had some reasonably direct experience
with watching, I suppose one example of what

00:56:08.460 --> 00:56:12.420
you described, which is Automattic and very
good friend Matt Mullenweg.

00:56:12.420 --> 00:56:16.400
I'm an advisor to Automattic, and for those
who don't know, Matt, who's been on the show

00:56:16.400 --> 00:56:22.700
multiple times, was a lead developer of WordPress,
very familiar with open source, and then has

00:56:22.700 --> 00:56:31.470
built a sort of services and enterprise grade
secure, stable solution for many different

00:56:31.470 --> 00:56:34.890
businesses and people in the form of Automattic.

00:56:34.890 --> 00:56:37.480
So I'm deeply interested in this, and I know
you are as well.

00:56:37.480 --> 00:56:39.599
You're a big fan of open source.

00:56:39.599 --> 00:56:44.010
What else do you think open source could be
applied to or might be applied to?

00:56:44.010 --> 00:56:45.010
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

00:56:45.010 --> 00:56:50.520
So first of all, I think people are relatively
familiar with the big individual software

00:56:50.520 --> 00:56:51.520
projects.

00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:55.350
Linux being the most well known.

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:56.789
It's over 20 years old now.

00:56:56.789 --> 00:57:02.440
It's clearly the most used operating system
in the world.

00:57:02.440 --> 00:57:09.700
And what people might not know about it is
a lot of scientists believe it's the most

00:57:09.700 --> 00:57:10.839
secure.

00:57:10.839 --> 00:57:12.010
And that's kind of counterintuitive.

00:57:12.010 --> 00:57:14.900
Oh, all the code's public, how could it be
the most secure?

00:57:14.900 --> 00:57:19.700
But it gets beat up the most, because it's
used the most.

00:57:19.700 --> 00:57:26.070
There's a great piece of writing, an incredible
piece of writing called The Cathedral and

00:57:26.070 --> 00:57:34.450
the Bazaar that was the first kind of magnum
opus on why this open source thing might work.

00:57:34.450 --> 00:57:41.570
And another thing people don't get is The
Cathedral and the Bazaar compared– Like,

00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:51.190
are you trying to build up the Gaudí's Church
or Bizarre, which is super flat and wide and

00:57:51.190 --> 00:57:52.730
obviously built by lots of people.

00:57:52.730 --> 00:57:58.770
And the thing is, open source is way better
at complex problems than simple problems.

00:57:58.770 --> 00:58:04.280
And it's the very complex problems that'd
be hard for a single company to do.

00:58:04.280 --> 00:58:09.410
And so if you're building an operating system,
it might have all kind of edges on how it

00:58:09.410 --> 00:58:17.390
integrates with other systems, different drivers
you might need, and the world is able to build

00:58:17.390 --> 00:58:21.470
that, whereas an individual company would
be very difficult.

00:58:21.470 --> 00:58:27.221
And so we started to see that in MySQL and
there've been others like MongoDB and we're

00:58:27.221 --> 00:58:31.730
in one called Elastic and there've been a
lot of successful companies around single

00:58:31.730 --> 00:58:34.990
software frameworks.

00:58:34.990 --> 00:58:40.800
Something started happening about 10 to 15
years ago, which is people started using open

00:58:40.800 --> 00:58:46.500
source in more complex ways and in sometimes
using it defensively rather than offensively.

00:58:46.500 --> 00:58:50.579
So the most well known is probably Android.

00:58:50.579 --> 00:58:56.809
So Apple had come out with this smartphone,
you could only get it on AT&amp;T.

00:58:56.809 --> 00:59:01.520
It's scared the shit out of everybody, not
just Google, but it's scared the out of all

00:59:01.520 --> 00:59:02.970
the other telcos.

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:05.920
It's scared the out of all the other handset
manufacturers.

00:59:05.920 --> 00:59:11.050
And so Google did this clever thing they said,
"Oh, we're going to create a competitor, but

00:59:11.050 --> 00:59:13.869
it's going to be open source so you can trust
us."

00:59:13.869 --> 00:59:19.809
Now there's different versions of how open
something is, and Android's not very open,

00:59:19.809 --> 00:59:26.329
but at the time, compared to the threat of
Apple, it seemed like a much better trade.

00:59:26.329 --> 00:59:31.680
And so they got the world behind them on this
thing, and it took off.

00:59:31.680 --> 00:59:37.540
Since then, that model's been repeated in
some esoteric ways.

00:59:37.540 --> 00:59:43.480
Facebook has something called The Open Compute
Project and if you want your hardware to go

00:59:43.480 --> 00:59:50.119
into the Facebook data server room, it has
to be compatible with their open source framework.

00:59:50.119 --> 00:59:58.099
And what that means is no one can have an
IP claim against Facebook because you've basically

00:59:58.099 --> 01:00:00.010
sworn off your IP.

01:00:00.010 --> 01:00:03.339
So they're commoditizing the stuff they're
going to purchase.

01:00:03.339 --> 01:00:09.640
Since then, AT&amp;T and China Mobile have worked
with The Linux Foundation to do the same thing

01:00:09.640 --> 01:00:12.789
for the wireless and wire line equipment stack.

01:00:12.789 --> 01:00:16.710
So they're defining open standards for their
next gen products.

01:00:16.710 --> 01:00:23.070
And if you're a supplier that wants to sell
to them, you have to agree to those standards.

01:00:23.070 --> 01:00:27.109
And once again, you're out of the IP game
so they don't get held up.

01:00:27.109 --> 01:00:28.119
It's very clever stuff.

01:00:28.119 --> 01:00:29.940
Tim Ferriss: Would you mind just expanding
on that a little bit?

01:00:29.940 --> 01:00:30.940
The IP piece?

01:00:30.940 --> 01:00:34.380
Bill Gurley: Well, let's go to the Facebook
one because it's more understandable.

01:00:34.380 --> 01:00:37.170
It's called the Open Compute Foundation.

01:00:37.170 --> 01:00:42.710
It's managed by the Linux Foundation acts
as a steward much in the way that the crypto

01:00:42.710 --> 01:00:47.750
world thinks DAO manages this loose federation.

01:00:47.750 --> 01:00:49.339
The Linux Foundation does that.

01:00:49.339 --> 01:00:53.289
Linux has been a DAO for 25 years, it's super
interesting.

01:00:53.289 --> 01:01:02.220
So that group, they're like a nonprofit overseer
of the project break ties.

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:05.760
They do patent defense, which I think is super
interesting.

01:01:05.760 --> 01:01:10.130
So they pull patents in, no one sues within
the open source project, but if someone were

01:01:10.130 --> 01:01:13.790
to come attack it, you'd go out after.

01:01:13.790 --> 01:01:18.770
So anyway, Facebook has all these equipment
in their data center, networking equipment,

01:01:18.770 --> 01:01:21.190
storage equipment, computers, software.

01:01:21.190 --> 01:01:26.270
So they just created this thing called the
Open Compute Foundation that defines open

01:01:26.270 --> 01:01:29.410
standards for how all these products work.

01:01:29.410 --> 01:01:35.089
And so if you want to be on their purchasing
list, you say, "Yes, we are compatible with

01:01:35.089 --> 01:01:36.720
this open standard."

01:01:36.720 --> 01:01:39.390
And once again, it basically commoditizes
that equipment.

01:01:39.390 --> 01:01:42.140
And once again, so —
Tim Ferriss: It just gives Facebook more leverage

01:01:42.140 --> 01:01:43.140
with —
Bill Gurley: Yes but now —

01:01:43.140 --> 01:01:44.580
Tim Ferriss: A broader spectrum of suppliers.

01:01:44.580 --> 01:01:50.599
Bill Gurley: Every one of these things has
a website and you can go see and so now Amazon

01:01:50.599 --> 01:01:53.320
and Google, they're all a part of Open Compute.

01:01:53.320 --> 01:01:54.320
Tim Ferriss: I got it.

01:01:54.320 --> 01:01:57.289
If I'm understanding you correctly, tell me
if I'm missing something, they're trying to

01:01:57.289 --> 01:02:01.880
create conditions such that they wouldn't
end up back in the day when I moved to Silicon

01:02:01.880 --> 01:02:07.500
Valley, I worked in storage area networking,
and EMC at the time was pretty famous for

01:02:07.500 --> 01:02:08.980
being a black box.

01:02:08.980 --> 01:02:13.470
If anything broke, if anything went wrong,
you needed EMC to fix it or upgrade it and

01:02:13.470 --> 01:02:20.010
so to avoid being held hostage or losing leverage
with suppliers, Facebook made this move.

01:02:20.010 --> 01:02:21.010
Is that a fair description?

01:02:21.010 --> 01:02:22.010
Bill Gurley: Absolutely.

01:02:22.010 --> 01:02:23.990
And then another high profile one, Google
was very worried about Amazon.

01:02:23.990 --> 01:02:24.990
Tim Ferriss: It's genius.

01:02:24.990 --> 01:02:25.990
I mean, it's very smart.

01:02:25.990 --> 01:02:26.990
Bill Gurley: Oh, it's super clever.

01:02:26.990 --> 01:02:28.750
Amazon was very afraid of — I mean Google
was very afraid of Amazon running away with

01:02:28.750 --> 01:02:32.710
the cloud services business in AWS.

01:02:32.710 --> 01:02:38.460
And so they had a piece of technology called
Kubernetes, and this was right around when

01:02:38.460 --> 01:02:43.539
Docker and containerization took off, and
Kubernetes was an orchestration layer for

01:02:43.539 --> 01:02:44.539
containers.

01:02:44.539 --> 01:02:46.920
And Google decided, Hey —
Tim Ferriss: What a bizarre name.

01:02:46.920 --> 01:02:47.920
What was it?

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:48.920
Kubernetes?

01:02:48.920 --> 01:02:49.920
Sounds like an Italian pasta.

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:50.920
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:02:50.920 --> 01:02:51.920
Well, here's the thing though.

01:02:51.920 --> 01:02:57.049
They decided that it was in their best interest
to take this technology and gift it to an

01:02:57.049 --> 01:02:59.310
open source consortium.

01:02:59.310 --> 01:03:06.530
They got the Linux Foundation to manage it,
and they went out and recruited IBM and HP

01:03:06.530 --> 01:03:11.839
and all these other vendors to say, "Oh, yeah,
we'll support Kubernetes," because everyone

01:03:11.839 --> 01:03:16.839
wanted to make sure that people weren't locked
in to Amazon.

01:03:16.839 --> 01:03:22.880
And eventually it got so successful that Amazon
had to announce support for Kubernetes.

01:03:22.880 --> 01:03:29.160
And now if you need to move a workload from
Google to Amazon, there's a common framework

01:03:29.160 --> 01:03:31.340
for which to do that and so you're less locked
in.

01:03:31.340 --> 01:03:36.970
Tim Ferriss: So this seems like it's become,
at least based on the examples you gave, a

01:03:36.970 --> 01:03:43.230
very refined, reliable counterpunch that Google
uses.

01:03:43.230 --> 01:03:45.940
Bill Gurley: I call it defensive corporate
strategy.

01:03:45.940 --> 01:03:46.940
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:03:46.940 --> 01:03:49.369
Bill Gurley: I'll give you two more very recently.

01:03:49.369 --> 01:03:51.770
Well, there's always been OpenStreetMaps.

01:03:51.770 --> 01:03:56.490
And that's kind of interesting because it's
data rather than — but the OpenStreetMap

01:03:56.490 --> 01:04:00.589
team was very kind of non-commercial.

01:04:00.589 --> 01:04:06.130
So they didn't like when Apple — and Google's
obviously running away with the maps business

01:04:06.130 --> 01:04:11.369
so if you just look this up within the past
four weeks, there's a new open source map

01:04:11.369 --> 01:04:19.529
group that's going to live on top of OpenStreetMap
that has Facebook and Microsoft and the other

01:04:19.529 --> 01:04:22.310
parties that don't want Google to run away
with it.

01:04:22.310 --> 01:04:25.319
And quite frankly, I think that's pretty cool.

01:04:25.319 --> 01:04:29.150
If you talk to — actually, someone you should
have on if I think that would be really cool

01:04:29.150 --> 01:04:32.980
is Jim Zemlin, who's run The Linux Foundation
for this whole time.

01:04:32.980 --> 01:04:34.160
Tim Ferriss: Oh, good idea.

01:04:34.160 --> 01:04:39.490
Bill Gurley: He's a huge believer and this
kind of ties into Matt Ridley's "ideas having

01:04:39.490 --> 01:04:47.200
sex" that not having IP is actually great
for the world because it just creates constraints

01:04:47.200 --> 01:04:51.190
for people being able to take advantage of
things.

01:04:51.190 --> 01:04:55.820
And so mapping’s super interesting to me
because it's data oriented.

01:04:55.820 --> 01:04:59.819
There's something called RISC-V, which is
an open source processor, believe it or not,

01:04:59.819 --> 01:05:01.880
that has a lot of momentum now.

01:05:01.880 --> 01:05:06.809
You'll see people on the earnings calls for
ARM, they'll start, “Is this a competitor?

01:05:06.809 --> 01:05:07.980
Is this going to be a problem?”

01:05:07.980 --> 01:05:14.470
Because it's completely free licensed and
China is a big backer of open source, and

01:05:14.470 --> 01:05:18.799
you could understand why because the West
has accused them of IP thefts.

01:05:18.799 --> 01:05:21.349
So this is where you can't be accused.

01:05:21.349 --> 01:05:22.349
Tim Ferriss: Safe space.

01:05:22.349 --> 01:05:23.349
So they– Safe space.

01:05:23.349 --> 01:05:24.420
Bill Gurley: They're investing like crazy.

01:05:24.420 --> 01:05:25.980
But I wonder what's possible.

01:05:25.980 --> 01:05:28.140
I'll give you some kind of grander ideas.

01:05:28.140 --> 01:05:32.020
First of all, I think autonomous vehicles
should definitely be open source.

01:05:32.020 --> 01:05:37.480
I think everyone benefits, safety's higher,
communication layers are better.

01:05:37.480 --> 01:05:42.510
The idea that you would use artificial intelligence
to figure out whether a light is red, yellow,

01:05:42.510 --> 01:05:46.170
or green is really stupid because it's a state
machine.

01:05:46.170 --> 01:05:51.359
It is in one of those three states and that
could be communicated into the software.

01:05:51.359 --> 01:05:55.859
You don't need to infer that, that's a known
thing, but you need a common language.

01:05:55.859 --> 01:05:59.099
And your test suites could all be used by
everyone.

01:05:59.099 --> 01:06:04.240
Academia could be working on the same thing
that the corporations are, safety's higher,

01:06:04.240 --> 01:06:08.130
easier for government to get involved if it's
a single standard.

01:06:08.130 --> 01:06:14.339
And I wonder about other things like the NIH
gives out $40 billion a year and many of these

01:06:14.339 --> 01:06:22.289
projects end up as research that leads to
a drug that leads to having a 17-year patent

01:06:22.289 --> 01:06:27.000
life and gets sold at 300 grand a year or
whatever.

01:06:27.000 --> 01:06:31.079
Why wouldn't we say, if you take NIH dollars,
your research is open source?

01:06:31.079 --> 01:06:34.599
Why do we use government dollars to fund stuff
that becomes proprietary?

01:06:34.599 --> 01:06:36.750
That didn't make any sense to me.

01:06:36.750 --> 01:06:37.750
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:06:37.750 --> 01:06:41.750
This is something that you and I can have
a conversation about separately.

01:06:41.750 --> 01:06:48.359
Bill Gurley: I look at the state of nuclear
energy where the costs are high because of

01:06:48.359 --> 01:06:55.680
regulation, not because of the actual product
and I wonder, what if the globe had a standard

01:06:55.680 --> 01:06:58.520
for fission-based nuclear reactor?

01:06:58.520 --> 01:07:03.390
Wouldn't we get to lower price points, wouldn't
we get to safer products, wouldn't they get

01:07:03.390 --> 01:07:04.579
more involved?

01:07:04.579 --> 01:07:05.920
It seems possible to me.

01:07:05.920 --> 01:07:09.710
Tim Ferriss: Are there any companies that
are on a short list of companies you would

01:07:09.710 --> 01:07:14.050
love to see exist or types of companies, like
anything that's just kind of a bee in your

01:07:14.050 --> 01:07:17.390
bonnet or has been for any period of time?

01:07:17.390 --> 01:07:18.799
Bill Gurley: Yeah I can give you a few.

01:07:18.799 --> 01:07:23.440
So, and this one's kind of well known in the
venture industry, but everybody talks about

01:07:23.440 --> 01:07:29.710
something called the interest graph, and they
wonder why there isn't an internet website

01:07:29.710 --> 01:07:34.780
that kind of links everyone that is tied to
a specific interest.

01:07:34.780 --> 01:07:41.631
And there are companies that people talk about
as being close, like Pinterest or Quora or

01:07:41.631 --> 01:07:46.380
Twitter, but I don't think any of them have
really pulled it off.

01:07:46.380 --> 01:07:48.200
Tim Ferriss: Would Reddit fall anywhere?

01:07:48.200 --> 01:07:49.520
Bill Gurley: Sure.

01:07:49.520 --> 01:07:56.250
Especially with some of the subreddits they're
getting close and people talk about kind of

01:07:56.250 --> 01:08:05.240
a holy grail, but there's not anyone that's
I think just really nailed it, really, really

01:08:05.240 --> 01:08:07.260
nailed it.

01:08:07.260 --> 01:08:13.820
And if you did, you would have this combination
of really cool unlock for people because you'd

01:08:13.820 --> 01:08:18.270
get connect– if you were into quilting,
you'd be immediately connected with everyone

01:08:18.270 --> 01:08:21.680
else that's on your level and you could imagine
that kind of thing.

01:08:21.680 --> 01:08:27.219
But then the advertising performance would
just be off the charts because you've kind

01:08:27.219 --> 01:08:29.859
of bucketed everyone into these places.

01:08:29.859 --> 01:08:34.400
I actually think Twitter still has a huge
opportunity on this front, but it'd have to

01:08:34.400 --> 01:08:39.080
build a top-down version of Twitter rather
than this feed thing, which is super hard

01:08:39.080 --> 01:08:40.080
for a lot of people.

01:08:40.080 --> 01:08:41.490
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by top-down?

01:08:41.490 --> 01:08:46.710
Bill Gurley: I think you could take all of
the information that's flowing in Twitter

01:08:46.710 --> 01:08:52.460
and all of the influencers that are in there
and build an algorithm that would score who's

01:08:52.460 --> 01:08:55.690
smart about certain things.

01:08:55.690 --> 01:08:58.199
Let's make it super simple to convey the point.

01:08:58.199 --> 01:09:04.290
If you look at stocks, so they already have
a new idea, unique individual identifier for

01:09:04.290 --> 01:09:07.290
each stock with the dollar symbol thing.

01:09:07.290 --> 01:09:12.580
But right now, you could do a search, but
there's a ton of noise.

01:09:12.580 --> 01:09:19.670
What if you had a page where, for each stock,
you had a list of the top stories of the day,

01:09:19.670 --> 01:09:26.259
and that could be pulled out of the Twitter
feed by knowing which people are the axes

01:09:26.259 --> 01:09:30.960
on that individual stock, which you could
infer simply with the data that's already

01:09:30.960 --> 01:09:31.960
in there.

01:09:31.960 --> 01:09:33.700
And so you could imagine that for sports teams.

01:09:33.700 --> 01:09:40.730
So I could have Twitter sports that is a top-down
version that's using the information in the

01:09:40.730 --> 01:09:45.520
feed, but then presents it more like a standard
newspaper would, if you understand what I'm

01:09:45.520 --> 01:09:46.520
getting at.

01:09:46.520 --> 01:09:47.520
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:09:47.520 --> 01:09:48.520
Yeah, I think I do.

01:09:48.520 --> 01:09:49.520
Bill Gurley: So anyway, that one's one.

01:09:49.520 --> 01:09:55.800
I'm highly interested in people, feel free
to reach out in any variation or new take

01:09:55.800 --> 01:09:57.020
on LinkedIn.

01:09:57.020 --> 01:10:01.900
I just think it kind of stopped and it stopped
10 years ago.

01:10:01.900 --> 01:10:05.020
One idea that —
Tim Ferriss: Aren't you semi-retired, Bill?

01:10:05.020 --> 01:10:06.090
Aren't you semi-retired?

01:10:06.090 --> 01:10:08.530
Bill Gurley: People will probably steal your
—

01:10:08.530 --> 01:10:10.610
Tim Ferriss: Your version of retirement is
super intense.

01:10:10.610 --> 01:10:11.610
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:10:11.610 --> 01:10:14.840
People may steal this and that's okay, but
I hope they reach out to me.

01:10:14.840 --> 01:10:21.090
It feels to me like you could have page rank
for people.

01:10:21.090 --> 01:10:26.460
And so I don't think the skill thing on LinkedIn
works because it's public so you get all this

01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:30.820
performance art where people are just being
nice.

01:10:30.820 --> 01:10:37.820
But what if you had people maybe privately
opining on who they think is smartest on particular

01:10:37.820 --> 01:10:39.040
topics?

01:10:39.040 --> 01:10:45.360
You can develop a really cool product that
would be unique to each individual.

01:10:45.360 --> 01:10:49.900
Because what you see, Tim, would be different
than what I see because it starts by who you

01:10:49.900 --> 01:10:55.600
trust and then what you see is there — anyway,
I think that's a really interesting concept.

01:10:55.600 --> 01:11:04.420
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, I wonder if Twitter could
actually use the literal page rank by correlating

01:11:04.420 --> 01:11:08.780
sort of verified account, that's become kind
of unusable for me because it was kind of

01:11:08.780 --> 01:11:12.469
pay for play for a while so it's it difficult
to filter now.

01:11:12.469 --> 01:11:18.920
But if people have websites that are their
personal websites, just looking at the page

01:11:18.920 --> 01:11:23.120
rank, I mean seems like from a shooting side,
pretty easy to do.

01:11:23.120 --> 01:11:27.330
Bill Gurley: I think one thing Twitter could
do that would just be a super industry is

01:11:27.330 --> 01:11:31.900
create a leaderboard for every topic under
the sun.

01:11:31.900 --> 01:11:36.420
And then the things you could build once you
had that would be super compelling.

01:11:36.420 --> 01:11:38.290
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:11:38.290 --> 01:11:44.230
Let's talk about Twitter, specifically a tweet
thread, and feel free to amend this, but I'd

01:11:44.230 --> 01:11:48.750
love to have you walk me through this particular
thread.

01:11:48.750 --> 01:11:51.520
This is from spring of 2022.

01:11:51.520 --> 01:11:57.210
It's not that long ago, and here it goes.

01:11:57.210 --> 01:12:02.830
So there are four points under an intro and
I'll just read it if you don't mind.

01:12:02.830 --> 01:12:06.250
"An entire generation of entrepreneurs and
tech investors built their entire perspectives

01:12:06.250 --> 01:12:10.790
on valuation during the second half of a 13-year
amazing bull market run.

01:12:10.790 --> 01:12:15.040
The 'unlearning' process could be painful,
surprising, and unsettling to many.

01:12:15.040 --> 01:12:16.610
I anticipate denial."

01:12:16.610 --> 01:12:17.610
Number one.

01:12:17.610 --> 01:12:19.219
And maybe we could just do these point by
point.

01:12:19.219 --> 01:12:20.590
Well, we don't have to.

01:12:20.590 --> 01:12:22.239
I'll do two at a time.

01:12:22.239 --> 01:12:24.449
"1) Previous 'all-time' highs are completely
irrelevant.

01:12:24.449 --> 01:12:28.440
It's not 'cheap' because it is down 70%.

01:12:28.440 --> 01:12:29.670
Forget those prices happened."

01:12:29.670 --> 01:12:32.840
Yeah, I screwed myself on that first one.

01:12:32.840 --> 01:12:36.270
"2) Valuation multiples are always a hack
proxy.

01:12:36.270 --> 01:12:37.270
Dangerous to use.

01:12:37.270 --> 01:12:40.500
If you insist, 10X should be considered AMAZING
and an upper limit.

01:12:40.500 --> 01:12:41.960
Over that, silly."

01:12:41.960 --> 01:12:44.620
So let's just sit on those two for a second.

01:12:44.620 --> 01:12:49.270
Would you mind just expanding on either or
both of those?

01:12:49.270 --> 01:12:56.310
Bill Gurley: There's an unfortunate reality
in the venture world that really became very

01:12:56.310 --> 01:12:59.940
crystal clear to me through a conversation
with Howard Marks, actually.

01:12:59.940 --> 01:13:02.949
But it's structurally set up.

01:13:02.949 --> 01:13:08.420
People talk about boom, bust, and cycles,
but this is set up more like a sawtooth.

01:13:08.420 --> 01:13:16.360
So risk on happens very slowly, almost like
the rollercoaster [makes sound of slowly climbing

01:13:16.360 --> 01:13:17.360
roller coaster].]

01:13:17.360 --> 01:13:23.090
But when it crashes, and if it's interesting
to you, I explain or do my best job of explaining

01:13:23.090 --> 01:13:28.790
why it's structured this way, when it crashes
it happens all at once so it's more like a

01:13:28.790 --> 01:13:35.800
sawtooth than a sine wave and it just crashes
and it's painful.

01:13:35.800 --> 01:13:43.350
And that just happened and it looks like it
happens every 7 to 15 years.

01:13:43.350 --> 01:13:46.750
And I thought this was going to happen six
years ago.

01:13:46.750 --> 01:13:47.870
I even wrote some things.

01:13:47.870 --> 01:13:49.750
I was way early.

01:13:49.750 --> 01:13:53.810
It took six more years and it got ridiculously
crazy.

01:13:53.810 --> 01:13:59.270
But it took so long from '09, which was the
last kind of — and '09 wasn't as hard a

01:13:59.270 --> 01:14:07.540
reset as '01 but from '09 to 2013, because
so many entrepreneurs are young, you had people

01:14:07.540 --> 01:14:14.830
grow up that had never seen a reset and risk
on is a lot like the bull frog.

01:14:14.830 --> 01:14:15.830
You don't know what's happening.

01:14:15.830 --> 01:14:17.840
You're just —
Tim Ferriss: Would you mind just quickly defining

01:14:17.840 --> 01:14:20.510
risk on for people listening, what do you
mean by that?

01:14:20.510 --> 01:14:27.960
Bill Gurley: So the community as a whole takes
on more risk gradually without realizing they're

01:14:27.960 --> 01:14:34.400
doing it and their mental models and their
frameworks adjust daily to what's happening.

01:14:34.400 --> 01:14:41.850
And so their thought about how the world works
is really a window of five years, or maybe

01:14:41.850 --> 01:14:45.230
three years, not 30 years.

01:14:45.230 --> 01:14:51.460
Because for many of them, they don't have
the 30-year perspective and when the going

01:14:51.460 --> 01:14:58.460
gets good, greed takes over and you weigh
the data points that feel good to you and

01:14:58.460 --> 01:15:00.800
are going to make you look —
Tim Ferriss: Yeah a lot of confirmation bias.

01:15:00.800 --> 01:15:01.800
Yeah.

01:15:01.800 --> 01:15:06.699
Bill Gurley: Oh, like crazy, and so then you've
shortened your window to the last 12 months.

01:15:06.699 --> 01:15:08.810
This is how the world works.

01:15:08.810 --> 01:15:15.540
And because things got so sloppy with interest
rates being near zero, speculation so high,

01:15:15.540 --> 01:15:21.840
money everywhere, we taught a lot of people
not only valuation things that will never

01:15:21.840 --> 01:15:26.460
be true again, but growth at all costs.

01:15:26.460 --> 01:15:30.421
Spend as much money as you can, you can raise
money every nine months if you want to because

01:15:30.421 --> 01:15:36.659
you could, the failure rate of companies in
the five years prior to this reset is probably

01:15:36.659 --> 01:15:41.090
the super low, probably the lowest it's ever
been, failure rate of startups just because

01:15:41.090 --> 01:15:42.960
it was so easy to raise money.

01:15:42.960 --> 01:15:49.990
And so you develop mental models and then
the world shifts dramatically, 180 degrees,

01:15:49.990 --> 01:15:51.530
whatever you want to say.

01:15:51.530 --> 01:15:58.810
It couldn't be more dramatic how fast it shifted
and even today, entrepreneurs will say, well,

01:15:58.810 --> 01:16:04.300
I just need to hold on until things get back
to normal and I'm not the only one.

01:16:04.300 --> 01:16:05.300
Tim Ferriss: Not normal.

01:16:05.300 --> 01:16:07.370
Bill Gurley: This is normal, dude.

01:16:07.370 --> 01:16:12.480
That was a fantasy you were in and you need
to forget it fast, but you can't.

01:16:12.480 --> 01:16:16.770
And there's another painful thing that I don't
even jest about because it creates real problems

01:16:16.770 --> 01:16:22.400
and it's actually quite unfortunate, but founders,
whatever that peak valuation is, they ran

01:16:22.400 --> 01:16:26.421
the math where they took their ownership,
they multiplied it by that number, and they

01:16:26.421 --> 01:16:29.660
thought about their net worth that way.

01:16:29.660 --> 01:16:34.639
And that can just be super destructive once
it's no longer true.

01:16:34.639 --> 01:16:37.130
It's just —
Tim Ferriss: You mean psychologically destructive?

01:16:37.130 --> 01:16:40.651
Bill Gurley: Psychologically yeah, I think
it's super difficult to come to terms with

01:16:40.651 --> 01:16:42.020
once you've been through that.

01:16:42.020 --> 01:16:46.370
Tim Ferriss: What do you mean by valuation
multiples are always a hack proxy?

01:16:46.370 --> 01:16:50.670
Bill Gurley: So this gets into some of the
earlier stuff we talked about, just about

01:16:50.670 --> 01:16:55.200
how deep you go on investing history and understanding
investors.

01:16:55.200 --> 01:17:00.170
But Silicon Valley, if there was a scale of
financial sophistication between one and 10,

01:17:00.170 --> 01:17:05.590
and you would say a really smart person in
New York is an 8.5, the average Silicon Valley

01:17:05.590 --> 01:17:10.830
person on financial literacy is a two.

01:17:10.830 --> 01:17:16.489
And it's funny because they make fun of Wall
Street, but it's just out of ignorance, they

01:17:16.489 --> 01:17:18.350
don't know anything.

01:17:18.350 --> 01:17:24.900
And so most of them think about valuation
by a price to revenue multiple, which couldn't

01:17:24.900 --> 01:17:26.620
be a cruder tool.

01:17:26.620 --> 01:17:33.680
And at one point I wrote a blog post called
"The Keys to the 10X Revenue Club," and I

01:17:33.680 --> 01:17:39.170
took all the public tech stocks and laid them
end to end based on price to revenue and one

01:17:39.170 --> 01:17:43.980
of them was at 20 and one of them was at 0.1
and it was just a big curve.

01:17:43.980 --> 01:17:46.241
So there's no line there.

01:17:46.241 --> 01:17:50.920
There's no reason to believe that that price
to revenue is how you should value anything.

01:17:50.920 --> 01:17:56.030
But just because it's easy, and these companies
are young and immature, it's hard to do DCF.

01:17:56.030 --> 01:17:58.260
It's hard to do something more sophisticated.

01:17:58.260 --> 01:18:01.130
It's the common language of the group.

01:18:01.130 --> 01:18:04.429
But things changed overnight.

01:18:04.429 --> 01:18:09.290
And so someone was pointing out, I think on
Twitter, that and they used price to gross

01:18:09.290 --> 01:18:10.929
margin instead of price revenue.

01:18:10.929 --> 01:18:17.750
Still, Twilio went from 70 times gross margin
to three in a very short window.

01:18:17.750 --> 01:18:22.980
I mean, talk about valuation reset, that is
just radical.

01:18:22.980 --> 01:18:30.560
And so it shakes the industry and no one's
going to feel sorry for Silicon Valley.

01:18:30.560 --> 01:18:36.000
And I'm not trying to elicit empathy, but
in terms of understanding what happens, it

01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:43.251
is so foundational, the change is so radical
that the best thing you can possibly have

01:18:43.251 --> 01:18:48.210
happen is if you can adjust your mental models
fast and get on with the new world, but it's

01:18:48.210 --> 01:18:49.300
very hard for people to do.

01:18:49.300 --> 01:18:55.770
And, by the way, I write that kind of stuff
in part to help the industry, and I'm super

01:18:55.770 --> 01:19:00.620
grateful, Sequoia in '09 put out, there's
a famous deck they put out "[R.I.P.]

01:19:00.620 --> 01:19:02.980
Good Times" or something like that.

01:19:02.980 --> 01:19:07.100
And these things help people adjust faster,
right?

01:19:07.100 --> 01:19:11.770
Having minimal models that — having structure,
having smart people tell them, "Okay," it

01:19:11.770 --> 01:19:12.770
gets them there faster.

01:19:12.770 --> 01:19:17.000
Tim Ferriss: So that I think relates to number
three, and you mentioned DCF, just for people

01:19:17.000 --> 01:19:20.280
who don't have that reference, discounted
cash flow.

01:19:20.280 --> 01:19:22.780
So number three is:
"3) You may be shocked to learn that people

01:19:22.780 --> 01:19:24.420
want to value your company on FCF…"

01:19:24.420 --> 01:19:26.170
That's free cash flow free.

01:19:26.170 --> 01:19:27.920
Am I getting that right?

01:19:27.920 --> 01:19:28.970
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:19:28.970 --> 01:19:30.720
Tim Ferriss: Yep. "...and earnings.

01:19:30.720 --> 01:19:33.030
Facebook trades at 14X GAAP EPS…"

01:19:33.030 --> 01:19:35.090
Am I pronouncing that correctly?

01:19:35.090 --> 01:19:36.139
Bill Gurley: Yes, yes.

01:19:36.139 --> 01:19:38.420
Tim Ferriss: Yes. "...and is growing 23%.

01:19:38.420 --> 01:19:40.880
What earnings multiple are you assuming?"

01:19:40.880 --> 01:19:44.179
So could you just walk us through that bullet?

01:19:44.179 --> 01:19:45.230
Bill Gurley: Yeah, yeah.

01:19:45.230 --> 01:19:48.540
No, I'm going to use a little bit of detail
so you can understand.

01:19:48.540 --> 01:19:51.044
But the point that — so if you think all
companies should —

01:19:51.044 --> 01:19:52.044
Tim Ferriss: I am, at best, at two on the
financial literacy.

01:19:52.044 --> 01:19:56.929
Bill Gurley: If you think all companies should
trade it like 10X revenue, here's one of the

01:19:56.929 --> 01:20:02.989
most successful companies of all time that
is producing massive amounts of positive cash

01:20:02.989 --> 01:20:10.260
flow and GAAP-audited earnings that's trading
at a very low multiple of those GAAP earnings.

01:20:10.260 --> 01:20:13.300
And earnings are a small percentage of your
revenue, right?

01:20:13.300 --> 01:20:18.100
So if you're trading at 10 times revenue,
you're probably trading at 50 times earnings.

01:20:18.100 --> 01:20:19.940
And here they're trading at 14.

01:20:19.940 --> 01:20:25.350
Or you may not even have earnings because
there's a great graph, we should try and find

01:20:25.350 --> 01:20:30.810
it one so that someone can see a link of the
percentage of companies at IPO that are profitable

01:20:30.810 --> 01:20:35.370
and it's this nice cyclical wave that goes
with these boom-bust cycles because Wall Street

01:20:35.370 --> 01:20:36.370
—
Tim Ferriss: We'll find it and put it in the

01:20:36.370 --> 01:20:37.370
show notes.

01:20:37.370 --> 01:20:40.370
Bill Gurley: So Wall Street does risk on also,
right?

01:20:40.370 --> 01:20:46.909
And so in really dark times, the percentage
of companies that are profitable is like 90,

01:20:46.909 --> 01:20:51.469
but by 2020, 2021, that number's five percent.

01:20:51.469 --> 01:20:55.500
The vast majority of companies are losing
money as they go public and Wall Street's

01:20:55.500 --> 01:20:57.210
encouraging that behavior.

01:20:57.210 --> 01:21:02.261
And so I've often said the venture like Wall
Street is the buyer of what venture capital

01:21:02.261 --> 01:21:08.040
produces and if Wall Street wants high-growth,
money-losing businesses, we will create as

01:21:08.040 --> 01:21:10.530
many as they can possibly consume.

01:21:10.530 --> 01:21:14.700
Because those are a lot easier to build than
the profitable ones, way easier, which is

01:21:14.700 --> 01:21:17.159
part of the cycle, that's part of why you
end up in this cycle thing.

01:21:17.159 --> 01:21:23.320
So anyway, the point of highlighting that
is just to try and get entrepreneurs and founders

01:21:23.320 --> 01:21:27.530
reset on where the world is today and where
valuations are.

01:21:27.530 --> 01:21:31.500
Tim Ferriss: I'm sorry to interrupt, and I'm
sure a lot of people listening will think

01:21:31.500 --> 01:21:34.710
this is a stupid question because they already
know the answer, but why did you highlight

01:21:34.710 --> 01:21:36.470
Facebook in the way that you highlighted Facebook?

01:21:36.470 --> 01:21:42.650
Bill Gurley: Just because I think on a historical
basis, Facebook is trading at a very low multiple.

01:21:42.650 --> 01:21:44.280
It looks like a very weak stock.

01:21:44.280 --> 01:21:51.380
I mean, Coca-Cola for years traded at 30,
35 times earnings, Facebook is at 14 — or

01:21:51.380 --> 01:21:54.470
Meta, whatever they like to be called.

01:21:54.470 --> 01:22:00.639
And 23 percent is a pretty impressive growth
rate for a company like this.

01:22:00.639 --> 01:22:01.639
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:22:01.639 --> 01:22:02.639
For sure.

01:22:02.639 --> 01:22:05.300
Bill Gurley: Coke is less than 10 percent
growth, like five or three.

01:22:05.300 --> 01:22:06.870
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:22:06.870 --> 01:22:11.820
Since you mentioned Meta, I mean on one level
I really have to admire the just doubling

01:22:11.820 --> 01:22:18.520
down on the Metaverse, what is your take on
this direction?

01:22:18.520 --> 01:22:23.870
Bill Gurley: So years ago, I read Snow Crash
when it came out.

01:22:23.870 --> 01:22:25.469
Tim Ferriss: Oh, so good.

01:22:25.469 --> 01:22:28.790
Bill Gurley: Thought it was the best thing
that I had ever consumed.

01:22:28.790 --> 01:22:31.611
And I was in hook, line, and sinker.

01:22:31.611 --> 01:22:37.130
so when Philip Rosedale started Second Life,
I was knocking on his door.

01:22:37.130 --> 01:22:39.480
I served on that board for 12 years.

01:22:39.480 --> 01:22:48.790
I have immense knowledge on this kind of immersive
stuff and Philip and I actually did a postmortem

01:22:48.790 --> 01:22:53.000
podcast recently, which I would point people
to.

01:22:53.000 --> 01:23:00.380
What we found is I think there's a difference
between the kind of gaming stuff and this

01:23:00.380 --> 01:23:09.340
idea that people want to live experiences
like they do in the real world in this virtual

01:23:09.340 --> 01:23:10.340
world.

01:23:10.340 --> 01:23:16.690
And what we learned in that second category,
and I see this in the Facebook demos and stuff,

01:23:16.690 --> 01:23:22.210
like, "Oh, we're going to do a board meeting
in-world," or whatever, is that that doesn't

01:23:22.210 --> 01:23:24.860
make a lot of sense.

01:23:24.860 --> 01:23:29.719
The number of people that love escapism, first
of all, young people do.

01:23:29.719 --> 01:23:34.929
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, you don't want to wait
in line at the postal office in your Second

01:23:34.929 --> 01:23:35.929
Life.

01:23:35.929 --> 01:23:36.929
Bill Gurley: Right.

01:23:36.929 --> 01:23:37.929
Well, but young people do it.

01:23:37.929 --> 01:23:41.170
They role-play a lot and so that makes sense.

01:23:41.170 --> 01:23:43.730
And then a handful of adults do it.

01:23:43.730 --> 01:23:50.330
They have wooden swords in the park or Burning
Man is that experience.

01:23:50.330 --> 01:23:54.240
But it's not a high percentage of humans.

01:23:54.240 --> 01:24:01.480
And one thing we found quite interestingly
is a lot of the people that love it are looking

01:24:01.480 --> 01:24:06.929
for an escape so they may actually have mental
health problems or they're in a tough spot

01:24:06.929 --> 01:24:07.929
in their life.

01:24:07.929 --> 01:24:13.320
And it reminds you that both Snow Crash and
Ready Player One were dystopian novels, right?

01:24:13.320 --> 01:24:14.560
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, totally.

01:24:14.560 --> 01:24:18.040
Bill Gurley: People were escaping a world
that sucked.

01:24:18.040 --> 01:24:23.940
And so we did a bunch of in world board meetings
there, and this is pre-Zoom.

01:24:23.940 --> 01:24:30.830
Zoom is an amazing substitute, which is one
of the frameworks from competitive strategy,

01:24:30.830 --> 01:24:33.330
to the notion of being in world.

01:24:33.330 --> 01:24:39.940
And so it's got even harder, because I think
Zoom's way better for a board meeting than

01:24:39.940 --> 01:24:43.410
making everyone get an avatar and sitting
around.

01:24:43.410 --> 01:24:46.540
I just don't think that's going to happen.

01:24:46.540 --> 01:24:51.540
So it's a long answer, but I think the premise
they have is wrong.

01:24:51.540 --> 01:24:55.420
I don't think this becomes the next platform,
the next smartphone.

01:24:55.420 --> 01:24:57.230
I don't see that.

01:24:57.230 --> 01:25:03.630
Tim Ferriss: So how do you reconcile that
with the position that they're trading cheaply?

01:25:03.630 --> 01:25:06.750
I guess that's just based on the math, but
is the —

01:25:06.750 --> 01:25:09.440
Bill Gurley: Oh, I think Wall Street's on
my side on this one.

01:25:09.440 --> 01:25:17.110
I think if they shut down the VR effort, not
only– Well, the profitability would soar

01:25:17.110 --> 01:25:21.590
because they're spending real money, like
five to 10 billion a year, but I think the

01:25:21.590 --> 01:25:23.100
stock doubles.

01:25:23.100 --> 01:25:24.100
Doubles.

01:25:24.100 --> 01:25:29.132
Tim Ferriss: Where would you suggest they
redirect their resources if they were to cancel

01:25:29.132 --> 01:25:30.460
—
Bill Gurley: I don't even take credit for

01:25:30.460 --> 01:25:33.409
this because people are saying it all over
Twitter.

01:25:33.409 --> 01:25:39.130
There's really good buzz on some of their
AI tools and the world's super excited about

01:25:39.130 --> 01:25:40.130
that.

01:25:40.130 --> 01:25:42.330
Tim Ferriss: Yes, they are, my God.

01:25:42.330 --> 01:25:46.540
Look at OpenAI and the Microsoft investment
and so on.

01:25:46.540 --> 01:25:47.540
It's incredible.

01:25:47.540 --> 01:25:50.420
Bill Gurley: I think it's a longer conversation,
but I think WhatsApp has some interesting

01:25:50.420 --> 01:25:51.420
things going on.

01:25:51.420 --> 01:25:52.420
What's happening there?

01:25:52.420 --> 01:25:53.420
Tim Ferriss: Did you give a teaser of what
you —

01:25:53.420 --> 01:25:58.610
Bill Gurley: Yeah, and if you read about WhatsApp
in India it has a similar place in the world

01:25:58.610 --> 01:26:06.219
that Tencent with WeChat does in China, they've
accomplished more.

01:26:06.219 --> 01:26:08.300
So those things are more interesting to me.

01:26:08.300 --> 01:26:14.792
Tim Ferriss: So I'm curious, I've had Mark
Zuckerberg on the podcast and he's a man of

01:26:14.792 --> 01:26:15.792
strong conviction.

01:26:15.792 --> 01:26:21.230
I'm curious from your perspective, like zero
to a hundred percent, where would you put

01:26:21.230 --> 01:26:27.980
the likelihood that he would ever can the
VR efforts and redirection?

01:26:27.980 --> 01:26:32.070
Bill Gurley: It's funny, a lot of people love
to discuss this, and what's that other–

01:26:32.070 --> 01:26:37.950
There's a bias when you get pot committed,
to me that's confirmation bias too, but if

01:26:37.950 --> 01:26:39.719
you've already bought something, you like
it way more.

01:26:39.719 --> 01:26:41.150
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yeah, sunk cost, well, sunk
cost fallacy.

01:26:41.150 --> 01:26:42.560
Bill Gurley: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:26:42.560 --> 01:26:43.830
It's super hard.

01:26:43.830 --> 01:26:48.470
And he's had people telling him what I just
said for two years now.

01:26:48.470 --> 01:26:54.960
So it's interesting because if you look at
other big bets like that, I think the two

01:26:54.960 --> 01:26:58.460
most amazing are AWS and Android.

01:26:58.460 --> 01:27:05.230
They were both kind of out of left field,
not part of their core business, but super

01:27:05.230 --> 01:27:06.230
successful.

01:27:06.230 --> 01:27:12.710
And I think you've already run the clock,
you've already spent way more than either

01:27:12.710 --> 01:27:16.530
of those did in the development of those things.

01:27:16.530 --> 01:27:19.969
And you just don't have the numbers.

01:27:19.969 --> 01:27:24.360
There was a thing going around Twitter that
the year-over-year sales of headsets is down

01:27:24.360 --> 01:27:27.150
globally for the industry.

01:27:27.150 --> 01:27:28.670
It's not happening.

01:27:28.670 --> 01:27:32.550
Tim Ferriss: All right, let's hop to number
four.

01:27:32.550 --> 01:27:35.190
"4) Revenue and earnings QUALITY matter."

01:27:35.190 --> 01:27:39.310
Could you please explain the word "quality?"

01:27:39.310 --> 01:27:42.270
Bill Gurley: Yeah, it's interesting that that's
like a nuanced thing.

01:27:42.270 --> 01:27:47.130
If you talk to someone who'd been investing
on Wall Street for 15 years, you could talk

01:27:47.130 --> 01:27:53.190
about revenue or earnings quality and they
would know exactly what you were talking about,

01:27:53.190 --> 01:27:55.670
but it's not something the average person
would know.

01:27:55.670 --> 01:28:03.500
And if that blog post again that I wrote called
"The Keys to the 10x Revenue Club," I go through

01:28:03.500 --> 01:28:07.650
12 different things that signify quality,
but —

01:28:07.650 --> 01:28:09.190
Tim Ferriss: And we'll link to that in the
show notes.

01:28:09.190 --> 01:28:11.640
Bill Gurley: Yeah, so I mean, a simple one
is margins.

01:28:11.640 --> 01:28:18.250
If you are reselling used cars and your revenue
is the price of the cars you're selling, but

01:28:18.250 --> 01:28:25.020
you're only making 10 percent on a car, that's
really low revenue quality compared to a SaaS

01:28:25.020 --> 01:28:27.580
vendor with 90 percent gross margins.

01:28:27.580 --> 01:28:35.260
When their incremental dollar of revenue creates
90 cents of gross margin, yours creates 7

01:28:35.260 --> 01:28:36.260
cents of gross margin.

01:28:36.260 --> 01:28:40.360
You can't value those companies both on price
to revenue.

01:28:40.360 --> 01:28:41.360
They're very different.

01:28:41.360 --> 01:28:43.970
So that would be revenue quality.

01:28:43.970 --> 01:28:47.880
Earning qualities typically relates to cash
flow.

01:28:47.880 --> 01:28:54.090
So you might have really good GAAP earnings,
but because of different factors in your business,

01:28:54.090 --> 01:28:56.739
your cash flows may not be nearly as good.

01:28:56.739 --> 01:28:59.360
It could be timing differences, those kind
of things.

01:28:59.360 --> 01:29:05.070
And so anyway, you'd go through the posts
that I wrote, but they're just elements of

01:29:05.070 --> 01:29:09.080
whether or not you have a competitive advantage
whether or not you have churn in your business,

01:29:09.080 --> 01:29:13.290
is this customer going to stay around forever
or they might leave tomorrow?

01:29:13.290 --> 01:29:19.369
One of the reasons Coca-Cola trades at a high
multiple is everyone imagines Coke will still

01:29:19.369 --> 01:29:21.469
be here 50 years from now.

01:29:21.469 --> 01:29:24.850
They have no reason not to believe that.

01:29:24.850 --> 01:29:28.460
Whereas some of these tech companies, why
is Facebook at 14?

01:29:28.460 --> 01:29:30.300
They don't know.

01:29:30.300 --> 01:29:35.210
Could TikTok take away their business and
then they're done overnight?

01:29:35.210 --> 01:29:39.560
Those become bigger risks for tech companies
sometimes because they look like they might

01:29:39.560 --> 01:29:40.659
be disruptable.

01:29:40.659 --> 01:29:45.670
Tim Ferriss: You mentioned, and you also write
about and speak about competitive advantage

01:29:45.670 --> 01:29:48.989
a lot, let me rephrase that question, what
are some lesser known or undervalued competitive

01:29:48.989 --> 01:29:49.989
advantages?

01:29:49.989 --> 01:29:53.110
Bill Gurley: Well, I mean we already talked
about network effect, that's one that just

01:29:53.110 --> 01:29:55.360
comes to mind right away.

01:29:55.360 --> 01:30:01.770
Lock in we had hinted at is there reasons
why switching costs, which is also it's in

01:30:01.770 --> 01:30:03.460
the Porter book.

01:30:03.460 --> 01:30:08.090
Are there switching costs that make it hard
to leave for a customer to leave?

01:30:08.090 --> 01:30:11.230
How many substitutes are there for your product?

01:30:11.230 --> 01:30:12.230
How unique is it?

01:30:12.230 --> 01:30:13.230
Right?

01:30:13.230 --> 01:30:14.230
That's a competitive advantage.

01:30:14.230 --> 01:30:15.590
It's like are you an N of one?

01:30:15.590 --> 01:30:18.690
And some of the network effect companies become
that way.

01:30:18.690 --> 01:30:21.790
I could create an Instagram competitor, but
I don't have everyone on it.

01:30:21.790 --> 01:30:28.659
So the user experience is a function of everyone
being on it so it's hard to compete with that

01:30:28.659 --> 01:30:29.659
thing.

01:30:29.659 --> 01:30:31.500
So you have a strong competitive advantage.

01:30:31.500 --> 01:30:37.050
It could be performance like an enterprise
product, you look at something like Snowflake.

01:30:37.050 --> 01:30:42.980
People just say, "This database does things
no other database will do," and so then that

01:30:42.980 --> 01:30:44.900
becomes a competitive advantage.

01:30:44.900 --> 01:30:52.580
But how hard is it for someone to find an
alternative to you and trade you out is an

01:30:52.580 --> 01:30:53.580
easy way of being.

01:30:53.580 --> 01:30:56.770
Tim Ferriss: This is not directly related
to competitive advantage, but I wanted to

01:30:56.770 --> 01:31:04.040
just revisit the total addressable market
and your OpenTable story with the spreadsheet

01:31:04.040 --> 01:31:09.530
or the model that had been capped at 17 percent
and you were like, "We're going to 99 percent,

01:31:09.530 --> 01:31:11.130
it's not 70 percent."

01:31:11.130 --> 01:31:20.590
Just to reflect back also on Uber, since you
and I were both along for that ride, pun intended,

01:31:20.590 --> 01:31:24.780
and in the early days there were three black
cars, two or three black cars in the very,

01:31:24.780 --> 01:31:28.020
very early days sort of prototyping.

01:31:28.020 --> 01:31:37.610
And I remember sitting with Garrett and looking
at some of the potential market sizes, but

01:31:37.610 --> 01:31:43.500
the assumption always was that the pie could
grow and should grow if Uber's functioning

01:31:43.500 --> 01:31:45.430
effectively and that's what ended up happening.

01:31:45.430 --> 01:31:51.830
So you can't say there are 700 black cars
in San Francisco, what percentage of those

01:31:51.830 --> 01:31:56.210
will use Uber, and the upper limit is 700
because then in the matter of a handful of

01:31:56.210 --> 01:32:00.989
years you have a thousand plus black cars
because of the rider demand.

01:32:00.989 --> 01:32:05.830
So I just wanted to mention that since you'd
also mentioned it earlier.

01:32:05.830 --> 01:32:12.050
Bill Gurley: Yeah, I wrote a very long piece
on this because there's a famous, I think

01:32:12.050 --> 01:32:18.210
NYU professor, his last name's not coming
to me right now, but he's famous for valuation

01:32:18.210 --> 01:32:19.210
work.

01:32:19.210 --> 01:32:25.510
He's always on CNBC and he wrote a piece that
said Uber wouldn't be worth more than, I don't

01:32:25.510 --> 01:32:30.670
know if it was two or three billion dollars
and I wrote a reply called “How to Miss

01:32:30.670 --> 01:32:31.670
by a Mile.”

01:32:31.670 --> 01:32:34.070
Tim Ferriss: Aswath Damodaran, I believe.

01:32:34.070 --> 01:32:36.830
Bill Gurley: Yes it is and he's wonderful.

01:32:36.830 --> 01:32:43.830
I called him before I published it just to
tell him it was coming, but it was a classic

01:32:43.830 --> 01:32:44.830
mistake.

01:32:44.830 --> 01:32:49.949
He basically took the taxi market and said,
"That's the upper limit," and that's just

01:32:49.949 --> 01:32:55.040
the wrong, like, math we made, or Travis and
the team made this thing so convenient and

01:32:55.040 --> 01:33:00.350
so available that it was a product that's
10x better than the taxi market.

01:33:00.350 --> 01:33:07.390
And by the time I wrote that, I already knew
that Uber in San Francisco was 20x bigger

01:33:07.390 --> 01:33:10.260
than the taxi market in San Francisco.

01:33:10.260 --> 01:33:12.360
So I already knew he was wrong with that analysis.

01:33:12.360 --> 01:33:16.480
We'd already belonged through it, but he didn't
know that.

01:33:16.480 --> 01:33:23.090
And I mentioned in that article, there's a
couple of classic examples of this, there

01:33:23.090 --> 01:33:28.469
was one where McKinsey was hired to calculate
the global market for mobile phones and came

01:33:28.469 --> 01:33:32.410
back with 900,000.

01:33:32.410 --> 01:33:39.199
And I have found you get into more trouble
with this kind of TAM conservatism, then it

01:33:39.199 --> 01:33:41.680
hurts you more than it helps you as an investor.

01:33:41.680 --> 01:33:48.119
If you feel like something's super disruptive
and it's unlocking things like your optionality

01:33:48.119 --> 01:33:50.750
to build on top of, that's going to be pretty
spectacular.

01:33:50.750 --> 01:33:56.480
Tim Ferriss: I was just going to say, it makes
me think of esports versus any comparable

01:33:56.480 --> 01:34:00.690
you might use with, I don't know, live viewership
of sports or whatever.

01:34:00.690 --> 01:34:05.170
It's just not the same thing.

01:34:05.170 --> 01:34:08.260
So that was the one through four points.

01:34:08.260 --> 01:34:18.239
Jeff Bezos, I think either retweeted or replied
to that tweet thread and with was very complimentary.

01:34:18.239 --> 01:34:20.490
You have spent time with Jeff.

01:34:20.490 --> 01:34:27.960
What do you think are some of the most underappreciated
aspects related to Jeff in any capacity?

01:34:27.960 --> 01:34:34.090
Bill Gurley: Well, I mean he's probably the
best entrepreneur that I've ever been around

01:34:34.090 --> 01:34:36.710
or got to know.

01:34:36.710 --> 01:34:41.560
It's remarkable and it's multifaceted.

01:34:41.560 --> 01:34:46.360
Here's one that I think is not well discussed
so he has a bunch of traits that make him

01:34:46.360 --> 01:34:50.330
a great entrepreneur.

01:34:50.330 --> 01:34:57.659
The company today is at such a radical scale
that there's no way, and I mean he's in the

01:34:57.659 --> 01:35:04.949
chairman role, he's not touching all the decisions,
he's not touching all the product decisions,

01:35:04.949 --> 01:35:12.740
he has built a organizational framework to
take what Jeff Bezos believes and run the

01:35:12.740 --> 01:35:19.020
whole company that way and that's not well
dissected, not well understood.

01:35:19.020 --> 01:35:25.619
But here's a great story, I'm riding in an
Uber, this is about eight or years ago, maybe

01:35:25.619 --> 01:35:27.850
seven, and I always talk to him.

01:35:27.850 --> 01:35:31.690
I always talk to the drivers because I'm a
shareholder and always talk to the driver

01:35:31.690 --> 01:35:35.860
and I'm asking him something about whether
we can stop.

01:35:35.860 --> 01:35:39.199
He goes, "Well, I've got to get back down
to San Jose by 2:30."

01:35:39.199 --> 01:35:41.389
And I'm like, "What happens at 2:30?"

01:35:41.389 --> 01:35:45.320
He goes, "I have to meet at the Amazon warehouse
at 2:30."

01:35:45.320 --> 01:35:46.949
I go, "What's going on?"

01:35:46.949 --> 01:35:54.179
He goes, "Oh, they've got this new program
they're running where you show up at 2:30

01:35:54.179 --> 01:35:59.110
and they have all these burner phones and
they load your car with packages and give

01:35:59.110 --> 01:36:02.390
you a manifest and then they booked a ride
over Uber."

01:36:02.390 --> 01:36:07.080
And so this was the early days of same-day
delivery.

01:36:07.080 --> 01:36:08.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that's cool.

01:36:08.710 --> 01:36:14.020
Bill Gurley: This is a company that's worked
tens, hundred billions of dollars that is

01:36:14.020 --> 01:36:21.900
running an experiment on top of Uber and I
know for a fact that most of the companies

01:36:21.900 --> 01:36:27.130
I've worked with that have gotten over 20
or 30 million in revenue would not run that

01:36:27.130 --> 01:36:32.770
experiment because someone would say, oh,
we won't know how to do the accounting, that's

01:36:32.770 --> 01:36:35.360
too much of a hack, like whatever.

01:36:35.360 --> 01:36:41.960
But this large company was super comfortable
running this kind of hack experiment on this

01:36:41.960 --> 01:36:44.420
other company.

01:36:44.420 --> 01:36:45.660
He showed me the manifest.

01:36:45.660 --> 01:36:47.270
I looked at all this stuff.

01:36:47.270 --> 01:36:51.960
Of course, I called Uber immediately thereafter
and briefed him that we were being used in

01:36:51.960 --> 01:36:56.730
a — but unbelievable, I mean, just unbelievable.

01:36:56.730 --> 01:37:00.920
No other large company would do that project,
none, zero.

01:37:00.920 --> 01:37:06.680
And so somehow he's institutionalized this
kind of experimentation and risk seeking,

01:37:06.680 --> 01:37:08.230
and he's talked about it.

01:37:08.230 --> 01:37:12.480
There's a great interview with him from Code
that you should try and find for the show

01:37:12.480 --> 01:37:18.730
notes from four or five years ago and I could
watch it over and over and over.

01:37:18.730 --> 01:37:22.360
It's like the Eagles documentary I could just
watch it again and again.

01:37:22.360 --> 01:37:23.360
But it's fascinating, someone asked him, "When
do you stop and experiment?"

01:37:23.360 --> 01:37:24.360
And he said, "When the last person with judgment
gives up."

01:37:24.360 --> 01:37:25.360
Tim Ferriss: Oh, when would he stop experimenting?

01:37:25.360 --> 01:37:26.360
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:37:26.360 --> 01:37:27.360
Tim Ferriss: Could you just say that one more
time?

01:37:27.360 --> 01:37:30.360
Bill Gurley: They ask him, "When does a internal
experiment get killed?"

01:37:30.360 --> 01:37:36.340
And he said, "When the last person with good
judgment gives up."

01:37:36.340 --> 01:37:38.540
And that's not how other big companies work.

01:37:38.540 --> 01:37:40.140
They don't run experiments that way.

01:37:40.140 --> 01:37:46.400
In fact, one of the reasons startups can compete
with big companies is because most big company

01:37:46.400 --> 01:37:49.699
experiments, they run one test and if it fails,
they quit.

01:37:49.699 --> 01:37:54.250
And a startup can't quit because they have
to shut down if they quit.

01:37:54.250 --> 01:37:59.530
So they run experiment one and two and three
and four and five, and then they pivot and

01:37:59.530 --> 01:38:04.710
do six and seven and eight, and they stay
up all night because it has to work.

01:38:04.710 --> 01:38:09.170
And so they just get way more shots on goal
than the big companies do.

01:38:09.170 --> 01:38:10.970
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, yeah.

01:38:10.970 --> 01:38:17.670
Bezos is also someone who has chronicled a
lot of us thinking and decision-making frameworks

01:38:17.670 --> 01:38:23.500
in letters to shareholders and there are some
compilations of his letters, much like Warren

01:38:23.500 --> 01:38:24.500
Buffett.

01:38:24.500 --> 01:38:25.500
Bill Gurley: Yeah, they're very good.

01:38:25.500 --> 01:38:31.250
Tim Ferriss: And they're very good just to
give one example, I mean it can be highly

01:38:31.250 --> 01:38:32.250
tactical.

01:38:32.250 --> 01:38:37.020
So I mean, the reason that people who would
call internal meetings would be required to

01:38:37.020 --> 01:38:41.060
put together, I think it was a six-page document
and the first 30 minutes of the meeting would

01:38:41.060 --> 01:38:46.660
be spent reading this meticulous document
and all of the reasons for why that was instituted.

01:38:46.660 --> 01:38:48.540
I mean, it's very concrete.

01:38:48.540 --> 01:38:53.000
It's not sort of ambiguous hand wavy stuff.

01:38:53.000 --> 01:38:56.130
So I definitely recommend people check that
out.

01:38:56.130 --> 01:38:57.170
And also —
Bill Gurley: By the way, mirror back to what

01:38:57.170 --> 01:39:01.750
we talked about earlier about writing and
thought process, if you're forced to write

01:39:01.750 --> 01:39:10.690
a six-page paper, it's much harder to put
that together than it is a five-page PowerPoint.

01:39:10.690 --> 01:39:14.270
It's easier to leave stuff out.

01:39:14.270 --> 01:39:16.659
You really have to think through everything.

01:39:16.659 --> 01:39:22.310
I mean, he's super curious beyond belief.

01:39:22.310 --> 01:39:29.100
He's willing to change his priors super fast
if he got something wrong.

01:39:29.100 --> 01:39:30.750
Yeah, it's something else.

01:39:30.750 --> 01:39:37.620
I mean, I think AWS is maybe top five business
move in the history of the world.

01:39:37.620 --> 01:39:42.060
I don't even know what — just the notion
that they launched that out of a consumer

01:39:42.060 --> 01:39:47.400
internet company and became one of the most
important enterprise companies it's fairly

01:39:47.400 --> 01:39:49.980
unprecedented, it's just amazing.

01:39:49.980 --> 01:39:52.130
Tim Ferriss: It is jaw dropping.

01:39:52.130 --> 01:39:57.580
And for people who want an Easter egg, if
you have not learned of this before, go to

01:39:57.580 --> 01:39:59.900
relentless.com and see what happens.

01:39:59.900 --> 01:40:04.760
It will forward directly to amazon.com and
that is not accidental.

01:40:04.760 --> 01:40:13.130
So is there anything else that you'd like
to say about the recent events and sort of

01:40:13.130 --> 01:40:18.880
correction/implosion or do you feel like you've
said what you would like to say, is there

01:40:18.880 --> 01:40:19.880
anything you'd like to add?

01:40:19.880 --> 01:40:24.070
Bill Gurley: One last comment I would make,
it was interesting, I was listening the other

01:40:24.070 --> 01:40:30.250
day to a podcast with Shane Battier, who I've
been fortunate enough to meet.

01:40:30.250 --> 01:40:31.340
He's the —
Tim Ferriss: I don't know who that is.

01:40:31.340 --> 01:40:36.310
Bill Gurley: Oh, he was famously out outlined
by Michael Lewis as the no stat all-star in

01:40:36.310 --> 01:40:37.660
The New York Times Magazine.

01:40:37.660 --> 01:40:44.920
He's an NBA player, really successful, that
kind of rode the analytics craze and did things

01:40:44.920 --> 01:40:49.920
that most people don't do, but when you ran
all the numbers, he's always winning and that

01:40:49.920 --> 01:40:50.920
kind of thing.

01:40:50.920 --> 01:40:55.840
And he played at Duke and has a ring from
the NBA.

01:40:55.840 --> 01:41:00.510
And anyway, he's now starting to dip his toe
into the corporate world.

01:41:00.510 --> 01:41:06.920
And so he did this podcast kind of a crossover,
but he was talking about Krzyzewski and how

01:41:06.920 --> 01:41:12.889
Krzyzewski, he spent four years there and
so he had a lot of stories, but I was listening

01:41:12.889 --> 01:41:18.719
and nodding because you've had great coaches
on, you've had great players on that play

01:41:18.719 --> 01:41:23.890
for great coaches, and these learnings can
be translated.

01:41:23.890 --> 01:41:31.699
And so he's saying, "Oh, Coach K. told us
that he expected the very must out of us.

01:41:31.699 --> 01:41:34.580
Each individual had to perform at their highest
level.

01:41:34.580 --> 01:41:36.880
He said, 'It's always team first.

01:41:36.880 --> 01:41:39.190
If you need to be an individual, you don't
need to be here.'"

01:41:39.190 --> 01:41:44.699
And Shane was talking about how people remember
the winners more than they remember whether

01:41:44.699 --> 01:41:47.250
you were the third or fourth scorer on a team.

01:41:47.250 --> 01:41:51.320
They remember the winners, and I think that's
true in startup world as well.

01:41:51.320 --> 01:41:55.300
And then three, he said, there's a singular
goal for this organization and it's to win

01:41:55.300 --> 01:41:56.810
the national championship.

01:41:56.810 --> 01:42:02.889
And those three tenants, he is talking about
relatives to Coach K. and I'm in the middle

01:42:02.889 --> 01:42:05.230
of nodding, and I was writing stuff down.

01:42:05.230 --> 01:42:06.730
I'm like, oh, I'll go talk to people.

01:42:06.730 --> 01:42:08.690
I'll go forward this podcast.

01:42:08.690 --> 01:42:16.310
And then I stopped cold and I realized in
2020, 2021, if an internet entrepreneur stood

01:42:16.310 --> 01:42:24.040
up and said those things out loud about what
he wanted from his company, he might get canceled.

01:42:24.040 --> 01:42:29.880
Company first, you have to perform at your
absolute best and the only goal here is the

01:42:29.880 --> 01:42:34.280
one goal of the corporation, we're all on
a team together.

01:42:34.280 --> 01:42:38.480
We wandered to a place that's very different
than that.

01:42:38.480 --> 01:42:44.270
And on your recent podcast with Jonathan,
he started to talk about the, I think you

01:42:44.270 --> 01:42:50.170
were using the word anti-fragility, but how
this had crossed over from the campus world,

01:42:50.170 --> 01:42:52.400
and these people were now —
Tim Ferriss: Right.

01:42:52.400 --> 01:42:56.949
I was asking him how to develop intellectual
anti-fragility, and he was giving the counter-example

01:42:56.949 --> 01:42:57.949
of what we've observed.

01:42:57.949 --> 01:42:58.949
Bill Gurley: Right.

01:42:58.949 --> 01:43:03.619
This happened in the university and this did
get out into the companies and so these companies

01:43:03.619 --> 01:43:10.369
were being basically held accountable for
delivering an experience for the individual.

01:43:10.369 --> 01:43:16.150
It's like your goal as a company is to help
the individual have a great life.

01:43:16.150 --> 01:43:20.290
And I think it's very hard to be high performant.

01:43:20.290 --> 01:43:26.199
Imagine if Coach K. had that problem, like
he had to make everyone make sure everyone

01:43:26.199 --> 01:43:30.340
on the team felt happy and safe and included
and that was true north.

01:43:30.340 --> 01:43:32.670
It would be very hard for him to be performing.

01:43:32.670 --> 01:43:34.869
I don't think he'd want to coach him.

01:43:34.869 --> 01:43:38.560
And we've lived through a little bit of a
reaction to this.

01:43:38.560 --> 01:43:44.460
So Brian Armstrong at Coinbase has very publicly
spoken.

01:43:44.460 --> 01:43:52.889
I think Tobi at Shopify took a little more
nuanced stance, but the same stance that like

01:43:52.889 --> 01:43:54.920
we have one objective here, which is this
company.

01:43:54.920 --> 01:44:00.371
And if you're hyper passionate about something
else that you got to talk about it all the

01:44:00.371 --> 01:44:04.700
time with this company, maybe you should go
do that.

01:44:04.700 --> 01:44:09.700
But this performance awareness had kind of
gone away because of everything I talked about.

01:44:09.700 --> 01:44:13.050
It was so easy to survive as a company.

01:44:13.050 --> 01:44:17.610
And now we're in a world where I think, you've
got to make hard choices, you've got to do

01:44:17.610 --> 01:44:19.869
what's right, you've got to perform.

01:44:19.869 --> 01:44:27.880
And so it'll be interesting to see whether
someone can act in this way without getting

01:44:27.880 --> 01:44:31.460
negative and Brian took a lot of heat for
what he did.

01:44:31.460 --> 01:44:32.460
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:44:32.460 --> 01:44:34.570
It seems to have passed pretty quickly.

01:44:34.570 --> 01:44:38.470
I mean, he was able to weather the storm and
take the heat, but it was pretty, I mean,

01:44:38.470 --> 01:44:39.470
what he did for everyone.

01:44:39.470 --> 01:44:43.730
Bill Gurley: I talk to executives at the bigger
companies are the ones we all know, they're

01:44:43.730 --> 01:44:46.040
dealing with this stuff on a daily basis.

01:44:46.040 --> 01:44:47.040
Tim Ferriss: Nonstop.

01:44:47.040 --> 01:44:48.040
Bill Gurley: Nonstop.

01:44:48.040 --> 01:44:51.550
And it's very similar to what's happening
at the university level.

01:44:51.550 --> 01:44:55.000
And so it is just super curious to me just
because I've always been one of these, I played

01:44:55.000 --> 01:44:57.620
sports in high school and college, and I've
always, when there's a winning team and the

01:44:57.620 --> 01:45:02.489
coach writes a book, I've always ran out and
read it and made it part of my menbtal model

01:45:02.489 --> 01:45:06.610
so it is kind of interesting to see where
we are.

01:45:06.610 --> 01:45:14.909
Tim Ferriss: What do you think the future
of, you tell me if this is a question with

01:45:14.909 --> 01:45:19.060
some there or not, but what do you think the
future of ecommerce looks like?

01:45:19.060 --> 01:45:21.050
And since you mentioned Tobi, I'm a huge fan
of Tobi.

01:45:21.050 --> 01:45:26.080
I mean, I was an advisor to Shopify super
early when they had about 10 employees, and

01:45:26.080 --> 01:45:28.730
I just think he's one of the most incredible
humans out there.

01:45:28.730 --> 01:45:33.050
But where do you think Shopify fits in or
doesn't fit into that?

01:45:33.050 --> 01:45:38.190
It seems like with some of the Apple privacy
changes and all these various things the landscape

01:45:38.190 --> 01:45:39.750
has changed super dramatically.

01:45:39.750 --> 01:45:47.040
If you try to look into your experience/crystal
ball to look forward at ecommerce —

01:45:47.040 --> 01:45:51.739
Bill Gurley: I'm a Tobi fan too, and at the
end of this, I'll tell a quick Tobi story

01:45:51.739 --> 01:45:58.000
that I think is a wonderful, a tool that people
can use and he gave me permission to share

01:45:58.000 --> 01:45:59.000
it.

01:45:59.000 --> 01:46:03.630
So I thought what they did with the shop app
was super cool.

01:46:03.630 --> 01:46:10.170
So the previous Christmas I started noticing
websites I'd never been to before, knew who

01:46:10.170 --> 01:46:13.360
I was and allowed one click checkout.

01:46:13.360 --> 01:46:22.690
And very few companies can make their business
as a B2B company and then have this crossover

01:46:22.690 --> 01:46:25.810
product, which has a consumer network effect.

01:46:25.810 --> 01:46:31.770
And they did that with that app and I thought
it was just super cool that they pulled that

01:46:31.770 --> 01:46:32.770
off.

01:46:32.770 --> 01:46:39.920
And so look, he is one of the truly greats,
and you've interviewed enough of these people

01:46:39.920 --> 01:46:43.830
like when you're with a Bezos or a Tobi, you
kind of know it, right?

01:46:43.830 --> 01:46:44.830
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:46:44.830 --> 01:46:45.830
It's different.

01:46:45.830 --> 01:46:46.830
And Tobi's been on the podcast.

01:46:46.830 --> 01:46:48.340
I recommend people check it out.

01:46:48.340 --> 01:46:49.530
Yeah, it's just a different feeling.

01:46:49.530 --> 01:46:50.530
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:46:50.530 --> 01:46:54.420
And they're hyper curious, they have their
own mental models, they're willing to learn

01:46:54.420 --> 01:46:58.699
new ones, they're constantly thinking.

01:46:58.699 --> 01:47:03.960
And they've built some type of organizational
fabric that's scaling, which is another element

01:47:03.960 --> 01:47:04.960
of.

01:47:04.960 --> 01:47:08.200
Here's the one he shared, which is one of
my favorite ever.

01:47:08.200 --> 01:47:14.170
So he said, "Whenever we're dealing with a
problem and we call a meeting to talk about

01:47:14.170 --> 01:47:17.420
the problem, I always start with this structure.

01:47:17.420 --> 01:47:21.000
We are here to solve a problem.

01:47:21.000 --> 01:47:28.230
So the one option that we know we're not going
to leave the room doing is the status quo.

01:47:28.230 --> 01:47:29.940
That is off the table.

01:47:29.940 --> 01:47:34.170
So whenever we finish this meeting, I want
to talk about what option we're taking, but

01:47:34.170 --> 01:47:36.520
it's not going to be what we're currently
doing."

01:47:36.520 --> 01:47:44.280
And I think that is brilliant because most
of the companies that I've ever worked with,

01:47:44.280 --> 01:47:47.310
their default is, "Well, let's not change
it.

01:47:47.310 --> 01:47:53.010
If we can't get the gumption to change something,
don't mess with it."

01:47:53.010 --> 01:47:54.480
And I thought it was genius.

01:47:54.480 --> 01:47:56.699
I just thought it was genius.

01:47:56.699 --> 01:48:06.230
Tim Ferriss: He's also, I mean, not only an
incredibly smart, effective human and leader,

01:48:06.230 --> 01:48:14.469
but a very humble, and I just admire him so
deeply, not just for the performance of the

01:48:14.469 --> 01:48:16.980
company, but for how he carries himself in
the world.

01:48:16.980 --> 01:48:22.860
I think it's just as impressive to me that
he carries that much horsepower but doesn't

01:48:22.860 --> 01:48:29.890
wield it in a overbearing way, which would
be very easy to do, a lot of people do it.

01:48:29.890 --> 01:48:30.890
So thanks.

01:48:30.890 --> 01:48:31.890
Thank you, Tobi.

01:48:31.890 --> 01:48:33.060
Bill Gurley: I agree.

01:48:33.060 --> 01:48:34.060
I agree.

01:48:34.060 --> 01:48:40.270
Tim Ferriss: So just a few more questions,
Bill and if this goes nowhere, I'll take the

01:48:40.270 --> 01:48:41.500
blame for it.

01:48:41.500 --> 01:48:48.110
So aside from the books that you have mentioned,
are there any books that you have gifted frequently

01:48:48.110 --> 01:48:49.110
to other people?

01:48:49.110 --> 01:48:50.220
Bill Gurley: Yes.

01:48:50.220 --> 01:48:53.400
There are three books that I'll mention.

01:48:53.400 --> 01:48:58.860
The first one that I've gifted the most is
called Complexity by Mitchell Waldrop, which

01:48:58.860 --> 01:49:03.590
is about the rise of the Santa Fe Institute.

01:49:03.590 --> 01:49:09.550
Even when you were talking with Haidt, I think
he mentioned certain things are complex adaptive

01:49:09.550 --> 01:49:10.550
systems.

01:49:10.550 --> 01:49:14.730
They're very hard to predict, they're very
hard to analyze, they're very hard to understand.

01:49:14.730 --> 01:49:17.060
That's what Santa Fe's all about.

01:49:17.060 --> 01:49:19.340
That book introduced me to network effects.

01:49:19.340 --> 01:49:24.730
When I get to go there, I usually go a couple
times a year and Mike Mauboussin's there and

01:49:24.730 --> 01:49:27.090
Josh Wolfe's there from Lockton.

01:49:27.090 --> 01:49:34.400
I just love the learning of things I don't
know, and just pushing my brain to go farther

01:49:34.400 --> 01:49:38.540
than I would with the normal stuff I consume.

01:49:38.540 --> 01:49:40.560
It's beautifully written.

01:49:40.560 --> 01:49:42.970
Great writer, Mr. Waldrop.

01:49:42.970 --> 01:49:46.080
There's a book that I happen to have right
here.

01:49:46.080 --> 01:49:49.800
This book, Mr. China is fantastic.

01:49:49.800 --> 01:49:56.449
He went to China in the mid '90s and started
a fund to privatize a bunch of industries.

01:49:56.449 --> 01:50:02.480
He's from London and got his head handed to
him and survived enough to write a humorous

01:50:02.480 --> 01:50:04.920
story about it.

01:50:04.920 --> 01:50:05.920
But holy shit.

01:50:05.920 --> 01:50:11.900
Tim Ferriss: It sounds kind of like the Red
Notice by Bill Browder equivalent, but in

01:50:11.900 --> 01:50:12.900
China.

01:50:12.900 --> 01:50:14.060
Bill Gurley: His life didn't end up getting
threatened.

01:50:14.060 --> 01:50:17.030
He did go to jail, but I read Red Notice.

01:50:17.030 --> 01:50:18.030
Tim Ferriss: There was a Red Notice.

01:50:18.030 --> 01:50:19.030
Yeah, yeah.

01:50:19.030 --> 01:50:20.030
Bill Gurley: This is more of a business —
Tim Ferriss: There's no death involved.

01:50:20.030 --> 01:50:24.360
Bill Gurley: This is more of a business book
and a know what you don't know and what can

01:50:24.360 --> 01:50:28.900
happen to you when you go into a foreign land.

01:50:28.900 --> 01:50:35.280
In the book that I've been fascinated with
the past five years, four or five years is

01:50:35.280 --> 01:50:37.770
Epstein's Range.

01:50:37.770 --> 01:50:42.469
David Epstein wrote a book called Range, which
it was kind of a counter punch to the 10,000-hour

01:50:42.469 --> 01:50:44.350
thing by Malcolm Gladwell.

01:50:44.350 --> 01:50:46.960
It started by talking about Federer.

01:50:46.960 --> 01:50:54.610
But at the end of it, he got into a couple
of notions that I find super interesting.

01:50:54.610 --> 01:50:59.520
A lot of the big breakthroughs in science
have come from people that have changed disciplines

01:50:59.520 --> 01:51:05.250
or changed genres, which you're talking about
a lot, which I just find super fascinating.

01:51:05.250 --> 01:51:09.980
If you go on Twitter, which obviously, there's
a lot of people shouting, but you'll constant

01:51:09.980 --> 01:51:11.860
refrain is like, "Shut up.

01:51:11.860 --> 01:51:13.540
You don't know anything about this field.

01:51:13.540 --> 01:51:16.770
Leave it to the people in the field."

01:51:16.770 --> 01:51:21.239
But if you study science and history, that's
not actually — the biggest breakthroughs

01:51:21.239 --> 01:51:27.710
come from people that had a different middle
framework and move over and then see things

01:51:27.710 --> 01:51:29.760
differently.

01:51:29.760 --> 01:51:32.119
I think it's super interesting.

01:51:32.119 --> 01:51:38.739
There's a professor at UCLA named Holyoak
who did a piece on something he calls far

01:51:38.739 --> 01:51:46.850
analogies where he views it as an intellectual
skill, but who can borrow ideas from farther

01:51:46.850 --> 01:51:49.880
away than where they are.

01:51:49.880 --> 01:51:57.190
Listen to some podcast of this person in this
field and have it impact what they do.

01:51:57.190 --> 01:52:00.199
I'm fascinated by everything in that world.

01:52:00.199 --> 01:52:05.450
Tim Ferriss: Range, for people who want a
little bit more, the subtitle is: Why Generalists

01:52:05.450 --> 01:52:07.570
Triumph in a Specialized World.

01:52:07.570 --> 01:52:13.590
And I did recognize the name because of another
book, which is The Sports Gene.

01:52:13.590 --> 01:52:14.590
Bill Gurley: Yeah, he wrote The Sports Gene.

01:52:14.590 --> 01:52:17.300
Tim Ferriss: Inside the Science of Extraordinary
Athletic Performance, which is a great book.

01:52:17.300 --> 01:52:18.300
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:52:18.300 --> 01:52:19.300
Tim Ferriss: The Santa Fe Institute.

01:52:19.300 --> 01:52:22.810
Let's talk about just for a second or maybe
a minute or maybe more than a few minutes,

01:52:22.810 --> 01:52:23.849
why is that important?

01:52:23.849 --> 01:52:30.480
I mean, I suppose explained it in part, and
what have you gleaned from your involvement?

01:52:30.480 --> 01:52:33.989
It seems like such another prominent commitment
on your part.

01:52:33.989 --> 01:52:34.989
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

01:52:34.989 --> 01:52:38.790
Another individual that's been involved there
for a long time and has been the biggest benefactor

01:52:38.790 --> 01:52:42.829
is a guy named Bill Miller, who was a very
famous Wall Street investor.

01:52:42.829 --> 01:52:48.820
Had one of the, he was at Legg Mason and had
a 15-year run where he'd beat the SNP.

01:52:48.820 --> 01:52:59.400
And I think for Michael, Bill, myself, and
Joshua, by listening and learning about analysis

01:52:59.400 --> 01:53:05.850
of these types of systems and seeing professors
from all the — another big thing about Santa

01:53:05.850 --> 01:53:11.400
Fe is that it's multidisciplinary, so they
have biologists hanging around with epidemiologists,

01:53:11.400 --> 01:53:16.179
hanging around with physicists, and they all
interact together.

01:53:16.179 --> 01:53:19.110
And Cormac McCarthy hangs out there too, which
is kind of cool.

01:53:19.110 --> 01:53:21.360
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, that is cool.

01:53:21.360 --> 01:53:24.800
Bill Gurley: And just hearing and learning,
you pick up things.

01:53:24.800 --> 01:53:25.800
It's hard for me.

01:53:25.800 --> 01:53:29.429
The network effect thing, I picked up the
first time I went there with Brian Arthur

01:53:29.429 --> 01:53:32.929
and I applied that quite directly.

01:53:32.929 --> 01:53:34.409
But there's other little things.

01:53:34.409 --> 01:53:40.139
There was a speech that was given three or
four years ago about the electric grid problems

01:53:40.139 --> 01:53:43.030
that were happening in North America.

01:53:43.030 --> 01:53:50.619
The professor that presented it at the end
said, "The best solution is smaller communities

01:53:50.619 --> 01:53:52.590
that are loosely coupled."

01:53:52.590 --> 01:53:57.670
And that is a really interesting — you could
use that framework.

01:53:57.670 --> 01:54:02.619
I walked up to her afterwards and I said,
"You just explained why the Euro's a bad idea,

01:54:02.619 --> 01:54:05.699
because you tightly coupled this thing too
much."

01:54:05.699 --> 01:54:11.010
And you can just imagine even a computer system
that's distributed, that learning could have

01:54:11.010 --> 01:54:12.270
other applications.

01:54:12.270 --> 01:54:15.290
If it's too distributed there's no scale.

01:54:15.290 --> 01:54:22.280
But if it's hyper integrated, then you have
this failure problem, this global failure.

01:54:22.280 --> 01:54:25.000
Anyway, that's an example.

01:54:25.000 --> 01:54:29.640
Tim Ferriss: Do they have talks and so on
online, or is it more of a close group type

01:54:29.640 --> 01:54:30.640
of experience?

01:54:30.640 --> 01:54:31.640
Bill Gurley: They do.

01:54:31.640 --> 01:54:32.640
There's lots of them online, you can see.

01:54:32.640 --> 01:54:33.640
Yeah, they do.

01:54:33.640 --> 01:54:34.640
They publish books.

01:54:34.640 --> 01:54:35.640
Tim Ferriss: Great.

01:54:35.640 --> 01:54:36.640
Bill Gurley: There's lots of stuff online.

01:54:36.640 --> 01:54:37.640
It's cool.

01:54:37.640 --> 01:54:40.030
Tim Ferriss: Okay, so last one or two questions.

01:54:40.030 --> 01:54:43.340
This one can go any number of directions,
but if you could put anything on a billboard,

01:54:43.340 --> 01:54:49.020
this is metaphorically speaking, just to get
a message, a question, a prompt suggestion,

01:54:49.020 --> 01:54:54.980
image, anything out to billions of people,
let's just assume they would all understand

01:54:54.980 --> 01:54:57.000
it.

01:54:57.000 --> 01:54:59.409
What might you put on that billboard?

01:54:59.409 --> 01:55:06.211
Bill Gurley: I think circa 2023 and everything
that's happened over the past five or six

01:55:06.211 --> 01:55:09.350
years, I would put “Be less tribal.”

01:55:09.350 --> 01:55:11.079
“Be less tribal.”

01:55:11.079 --> 01:55:21.060
And I have friends on both sides of the political
spectrum, and I can't imagine an activity

01:55:21.060 --> 01:55:26.969
that turns off more brain cells than tribal
affiliates.

01:55:26.969 --> 01:55:32.790
All the books that have been written on bias
and all the Kahneman [inaudible] and Thinking,

01:55:32.790 --> 01:55:36.520
Fast and Slow, and all the Nobel prizes for
that stuff.

01:55:36.520 --> 01:55:45.710
I think political bias is stronger than confirmation
bias, sunk cost, all those things.

01:55:45.710 --> 01:55:49.270
People are just turning off their brains and
it's both directions.

01:55:49.270 --> 01:55:53.690
And it's not just the fringe, it's not just
the populists.

01:55:53.690 --> 01:55:58.030
It's people that just affiliate and then they
don't think, they don't listen.

01:55:58.030 --> 01:55:59.150
And it's both sides.

01:55:59.150 --> 01:56:01.920
I mean, I'll give you an example.

01:56:01.920 --> 01:56:02.920
Gerrymandering.

01:56:02.920 --> 01:56:03.920
Horrible.

01:56:03.920 --> 01:56:04.920
They both do it.

01:56:04.920 --> 01:56:05.920
It's horrible.

01:56:05.920 --> 01:56:06.920
Yeah, just say it's horrible.

01:56:06.920 --> 01:56:07.920
But they don't.

01:56:07.920 --> 01:56:08.920
They say, "Oh, look.

01:56:08.920 --> 01:56:11.010
Those guys are horrible when they do it.

01:56:11.010 --> 01:56:12.400
Capture is horrible.

01:56:12.400 --> 01:56:16.139
And on the Republican side, that's banks and
big pharma.

01:56:16.139 --> 01:56:21.810
But on the other side it's unions, the teacher's
union and the police union.

01:56:21.810 --> 01:56:27.810
George Floyd doesn't happen if the police
union doesn't have the power they did because

01:56:27.810 --> 01:56:30.489
Derek Chauvin would've been off the force.

01:56:30.489 --> 01:56:32.800
But the police union protected him.

01:56:32.800 --> 01:56:34.369
And if you're on the left, you can't say that.

01:56:34.369 --> 01:56:35.820
You can't make that statement.

01:56:35.820 --> 01:56:37.060
You can't be anti-union.

01:56:37.060 --> 01:56:42.329
And it's just stupid for people to turn their
brains off this much.

01:56:42.329 --> 01:56:44.320
Anyway, I'm passionate about that.

01:56:44.320 --> 01:56:45.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

01:56:45.710 --> 01:56:53.909
How would you suggest — let's just say you
were teaching, you're a good teacher and you've

01:56:53.909 --> 01:56:56.110
spoken to students.

01:56:56.110 --> 01:57:03.660
If you were to give a presentation on cultivating
anti-tribalism, probably have a sexier headline

01:57:03.660 --> 01:57:04.660
to it.

01:57:04.660 --> 01:57:09.770
But what might that lecture or class contain
or blog post?

01:57:09.770 --> 01:57:15.719
Bill Gurley: I think the first thing I would
try and do is just highlight the fact that

01:57:15.719 --> 01:57:17.590
people are turning off their brains.

01:57:17.590 --> 01:57:24.679
The way that people have proven how confirmation
bias works, how loss aversion works.

01:57:24.679 --> 01:57:28.840
There's 20 different cognitive biases that
we're all aware of.

01:57:28.840 --> 01:57:33.060
The first thing I would do is just run some
— there's been stuff done.

01:57:33.060 --> 01:57:38.869
Then, why does anyone want to be intellectually
inconsistent?

01:57:38.869 --> 01:57:42.710
If you think it's an ends to a means, then
you're just in a fight.

01:57:42.710 --> 01:57:43.710
You're just in a fight.

01:57:43.710 --> 01:57:50.810
I don't even want to have a discussion with
someone if cheating on your spouse and philandering

01:57:50.810 --> 01:57:56.880
is okay when your side does it, but it's horrible
when the other side doesn't like, right.

01:57:56.880 --> 01:57:57.880
What's your principle?

01:57:57.880 --> 01:57:58.880
I don't understand.

01:57:58.880 --> 01:58:02.530
Anyway, I would try and get people in touch
with the fact that they're doing it.

01:58:02.530 --> 01:58:09.570
And then I would just highlight that our government's
far from perfect.

01:58:09.570 --> 01:58:16.010
Most tribal people view their side as near
perfect, and there's no sign of that anywhere.

01:58:16.010 --> 01:58:20.570
But there are a few orgs that I'm starting
to learn about.

01:58:20.570 --> 01:58:27.480
Nonprofits that give money to centrist candidates
or things like that.

01:58:27.480 --> 01:58:35.290
And I think the world, if you could go back
30 years, it was just more collegial across

01:58:35.290 --> 01:58:36.290
the aisle.

01:58:36.290 --> 01:58:39.560
Tim Ferriss: I mean, you've mentioned Twitter
a number of times.

01:58:39.560 --> 01:58:46.900
I feel like Twitter incentivizes for polarization
just with the mechanisms at work.

01:58:46.900 --> 01:58:48.590
And you're a good writer.

01:58:48.590 --> 01:58:57.310
I wonder why you post so much on Twitter,
which seems so impermanent compared to a blog

01:58:57.310 --> 01:58:58.310
post.

01:58:58.310 --> 01:59:03.520
Just the durability of the signal seems to
wane so quickly.

01:59:03.520 --> 01:59:09.040
Why do you now post more of your thoughts
to Twitter, say, than in longer form?

01:59:09.040 --> 01:59:13.639
Bill Gurley: Yeah, probably because I'm old
and I don't have the concentration that I

01:59:13.639 --> 01:59:17.350
used to have to get to the word out.

01:59:17.350 --> 01:59:19.120
You brought up two subjects.

01:59:19.120 --> 01:59:23.650
I'll answer the second part first, and then
we can talk about the tribal stuff.

01:59:23.650 --> 01:59:32.310
I find Twitter just to be super fascinating
as your ability to get close to experts, leaders

01:59:32.310 --> 01:59:34.710
in your field, like —
Tim Ferriss: 100 percent agree.

01:59:34.710 --> 01:59:35.710
100 percent.

01:59:35.710 --> 01:59:36.710
Bill Gurley: It's unbelievable.

01:59:36.710 --> 01:59:37.770
More so than LinkedIn.

01:59:37.770 --> 01:59:43.080
And there's a chance that some reply you give
to them, they might like or they might follow

01:59:43.080 --> 01:59:44.080
you.

01:59:44.080 --> 01:59:47.100
And now you, you've developed a mentor or
a peer partner.

01:59:47.100 --> 01:59:53.170
It's really just shocking, amazing the amount
of information you can take out of this thing

01:59:53.170 --> 01:59:54.170
if you use it properly.

01:59:54.170 --> 01:59:58.230
Tim Ferriss: I'd say a third of my podcast
guests come from Twitter interactions and

01:59:58.230 --> 02:00:00.500
then DM, which leads to —
Bill Gurley: Oh, yeah.

02:00:00.500 --> 02:00:05.840
I mean, that's something you and I may feel
that most people don't know, but if you develop

02:00:05.840 --> 02:00:11.860
a massive following, DM becomes this magical
place.

02:00:11.860 --> 02:00:16.360
About half the time someone asks me if I can
help them recruit someone, that person follows

02:00:16.360 --> 02:00:17.360
me.

02:00:17.360 --> 02:00:19.550
Now I can just start talking to them right
out of the gate.

02:00:19.550 --> 02:00:25.440
Tim Ferriss: There's a protection too for
high profile people in DM because there's

02:00:25.440 --> 02:00:30.800
no personal information that is exchanged
and they can always block you if they get

02:00:30.800 --> 02:00:32.370
sick of you.

02:00:32.370 --> 02:00:34.110
And therein lies the safety.

02:00:34.110 --> 02:00:37.869
You can also, if you're trying to interact,
and I don't do it that much, but interact

02:00:37.869 --> 02:00:38.869
with say a-list celebrities.

02:00:38.869 --> 02:00:44.630
Well, it's like, okay, you can try to wade
through this phalanx of agents and managers

02:00:44.630 --> 02:00:49.570
and publicists and just take two years to
try to get a message to someone.

02:00:49.570 --> 02:00:53.690
Or you can just DM them if that gate is open.

02:00:53.690 --> 02:00:59.030
Bill Gurley: There's a book idea that I'm
working on, and one of my core beliefs is

02:00:59.030 --> 02:01:06.630
that in this day and age circa 2023, your
ability to rise up in any industry or any

02:01:06.630 --> 02:01:13.090
particular endeavor is so much easier than
it ever was before because your ability to

02:01:13.090 --> 02:01:20.360
get close to the mentors, leaders, best practitioners,
and learn from them is unlike it's ever, ever

02:01:20.360 --> 02:01:21.760
been before.

02:01:21.760 --> 02:01:28.170
And add in hybrid work, maybe you can work
for a company that's not even near you.

02:01:28.170 --> 02:01:31.901
It's really awesome for people that want to
pull themselves up.

02:01:31.901 --> 02:01:32.901
On the tribal thing, yeah.

02:01:32.901 --> 02:01:37.179
Look, there's a lot of you and Haidt went
deep on this and he's way smarter on it than

02:01:37.179 --> 02:01:43.320
me, but it does reinforce performance and
yeah, it's too bad.

02:01:43.320 --> 02:01:45.210
Tim Ferriss: Yeah.

02:01:45.210 --> 02:01:47.730
Let's double-click on performance.

02:01:47.730 --> 02:01:52.389
Recent conversation with James Clear, and
I'm going to paraphrase what he said, "Who

02:01:52.389 --> 02:01:57.480
you are and the decisions you make are downstream
of the information you consume in a sense.

02:01:57.480 --> 02:02:00.230
So you should choose your sources of information
very carefully."

02:02:00.230 --> 02:02:03.540
And I would tend to agree with that.

02:02:03.540 --> 02:02:06.429
I'm pretty selective.

02:02:06.429 --> 02:02:12.659
Are there any newsletters you subscribe to
or read regularly or writers you follow regularly?

02:02:12.659 --> 02:02:16.050
Not in book form, but in more regular form.

02:02:16.050 --> 02:02:17.050
Frequent form.

02:02:17.050 --> 02:02:21.870
Bill Gurley: Eric Newcomer does a venture
capital Substack.

02:02:21.870 --> 02:02:26.590
And so just being in the industry, that's
something to follow.

02:02:26.590 --> 02:02:35.829
There's a website called Techmeme where Gabe
has this curated news rank, including Twitter

02:02:35.829 --> 02:02:38.340
comments people have made about that article.

02:02:38.340 --> 02:02:40.860
It's just, it's a daily —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, that's cool.

02:02:40.860 --> 02:02:41.949
I haven't seen that integrated.

02:02:41.949 --> 02:02:42.949
That's very cool.

02:02:42.949 --> 02:02:43.949
Bill Gurley: Oh, yeah, it's great.

02:02:43.949 --> 02:02:44.949
Tim Ferriss: Gabe's a machine.

02:02:44.949 --> 02:02:47.670
Bill Gurley: Yeah, it's really well done.

02:02:47.670 --> 02:02:51.690
In the industry, I go there.

02:02:51.690 --> 02:02:56.880
Between Techmeme and Twitter, I end up on
a lot of Substacks and whatnot, but I don't

02:02:56.880 --> 02:03:02.090
have one where I read every single thing they
post.

02:03:02.090 --> 02:03:08.270
Everything's been inverted into this consumption
world where the aggregator of Twitter or Techmeme

02:03:08.270 --> 02:03:13.690
is kind of funneling what I find and then
I go do those things.

02:03:13.690 --> 02:03:20.380
One of the things I think Twitter should do
is define a long-form bucket container, a

02:03:20.380 --> 02:03:27.520
podcast container and a video container, and
then have pocket like features where if you

02:03:27.520 --> 02:03:32.270
see something like that, you store it and
then it cues the way — I mean, that's one

02:03:32.270 --> 02:03:40.290
of the benefits of Spotify as a podcast listener,
I think it cues and organizes easier than

02:03:40.290 --> 02:03:41.290
some of the others.

02:03:41.290 --> 02:03:43.070
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, let's see.

02:03:43.070 --> 02:03:47.290
I'm just going to ask if there's anything
more you want to say about the book or do

02:03:47.290 --> 02:03:51.260
you want to keep that under wraps largely
for now?

02:03:51.260 --> 02:03:54.110
Is there anything else you'd like to say about
the work in progress?

02:03:54.110 --> 02:03:57.340
Bill Gurley: Yeah, there's a speech I gave
at the University of Texas that you could

02:03:57.340 --> 02:04:01.659
put in the show notes because it's out there
and it's talking about chasing your dream

02:04:01.659 --> 02:04:04.840
job and how to succeed and thrive at your
dream job.

02:04:04.840 --> 02:04:08.840
And I've done some research since I gave the
speech because people have encouraged me to

02:04:08.840 --> 02:04:10.239
turn it into a book.

02:04:10.239 --> 02:04:16.150
And some of the polling we've done show 70
percent of people have career regret.

02:04:16.150 --> 02:04:19.970
70 percent, which is a huge number.

02:04:19.970 --> 02:04:24.770
And so we're doing some more work to better
understand that and how people end up in that

02:04:24.770 --> 02:04:26.020
place.

02:04:26.020 --> 02:04:30.080
But the punchline, which I hinted at earlier
is I just don't think there's ever been a

02:04:30.080 --> 02:04:36.190
better time to have a self-determined job
process if you want.

02:04:36.190 --> 02:04:39.070
And the tools are better than they've ever
been.

02:04:39.070 --> 02:04:44.540
And I, there's way more detail in this thing,
but it was built off of studying the biography

02:04:44.540 --> 02:04:51.150
of a very unusual set of people, Bobby Knight,
Bob Dylan, and Danny Meyer, the restaurateur,

02:04:51.150 --> 02:04:54.880
and seeing similar patterns in how they attacked
what they did.

02:04:54.880 --> 02:04:59.420
Tim Ferriss: Just out of curiosity, if you
were invited to give and agreed to give a

02:04:59.420 --> 02:05:04.400
TED Talk, but you couldn't give it on venture
capital, couldn't do investing, couldn't do

02:05:04.400 --> 02:05:11.550
