Talk on?

02:05:11.550 --> 02:05:14.290
Bill Gurley: Probably regulatory capture.

02:05:14.290 --> 02:05:20.079
I have this core belief that capitalism and
democracy will eventually destroy one another.

02:05:20.079 --> 02:05:22.650
Tim Ferriss: Could you also just define regulatory
capture at some point?

02:05:22.650 --> 02:05:23.650
You don't have to do it right now.

02:05:23.650 --> 02:05:24.650
Bill Gurley: I do.

02:05:24.650 --> 02:05:26.590
I mean, there's a Wikipedia entry for it.

02:05:26.590 --> 02:05:27.830
Tim Ferriss: Just the basics for folks.

02:05:27.830 --> 02:05:32.270
Bill Gurley: There's a famous professor from
Chicago, and I forget his name, like 1958,

02:05:32.270 --> 02:05:34.079
that wrote the seminal report.

02:05:34.079 --> 02:05:40.829
But basically in a heavily regulated industry,
the incumbents typically end up being the

02:05:40.829 --> 02:05:45.809
ones that write the legislation and typically
lock themselves in.

02:05:45.809 --> 02:05:51.360
Build competitive advantage, build a moat
back to Michael Porter through the use of

02:05:51.360 --> 02:05:56.489
legislation, and make it harder for startups
to break in.

02:05:56.489 --> 02:06:02.409
One of my favorite examples, the Obama administration
came up with this program where they spent

02:06:02.409 --> 02:06:08.290
44 billion dollars paying doctors to implement
EHR systems.

02:06:08.290 --> 02:06:13.540
And the idea that you would pay someone to
implement software when you need to do it

02:06:13.540 --> 02:06:19.750
on your own for your own competitive survival
— but there was a healthcare advisory board,

02:06:19.750 --> 02:06:24.690
the CEO of Epic sat on that board and they
came up with this program.

02:06:24.690 --> 02:06:26.599
Tim Ferriss: What is Epic?

02:06:26.599 --> 02:06:29.940
Bill Gurley: It's the leading provider of
software.

02:06:29.940 --> 02:06:31.560
Tim Ferriss: Bingo.

02:06:31.560 --> 02:06:33.719
Bill Gurley: Yeah, exactly.

02:06:33.719 --> 02:06:38.760
Then, once they came up with this program,
the thing that you would obviously think is,

02:06:38.760 --> 02:06:41.480
well, what's wrong with paying someone to
use software?

02:06:41.480 --> 02:06:43.360
Well, they probably won't use it.

02:06:43.360 --> 02:06:49.980
Then they put up another 20 billion for the
second phase called meaningful use where if

02:06:49.980 --> 02:06:55.330
you proved you were using the software, you
got paid to implement, you get another check.

02:06:55.330 --> 02:07:01.360
It's mind numbing, and this stuff is everywhere
in our government, like I said, both sides

02:07:01.360 --> 02:07:02.670
of the aisle.

02:07:02.670 --> 02:07:03.810
Everywhere.

02:07:03.810 --> 02:07:06.560
And Citizens United did not help.

02:07:06.560 --> 02:07:09.940
It made it way worse.

02:07:09.940 --> 02:07:16.119
Our government would be way better if you
could somehow extract these powers from the

02:07:16.119 --> 02:07:17.119
government.

02:07:17.119 --> 02:07:22.750
Tim Ferriss: If you could wave a magic wand
and get some things moving to act as some

02:07:22.750 --> 02:07:28.849
countervailing force to reduce regulatory
capture, what would you do?

02:07:28.849 --> 02:07:29.849
What's the action?

02:07:29.849 --> 02:07:34.670
Bill Gurley: By the way, we have really broken
industries with the most regulated pharma,

02:07:34.670 --> 02:07:37.610
banks, telcos, these are the ones.

02:07:37.610 --> 02:07:40.889
I mean, look at the banks.

02:07:40.889 --> 02:07:47.800
Five other industrialized nations have moved
to insta transfer run by the government between

02:07:47.800 --> 02:07:48.800
banks.

02:07:48.800 --> 02:07:50.829
In the UK, it's called UK Faster Payments.

02:07:50.829 --> 02:07:52.760
It happened 17 years ago.

02:07:52.760 --> 02:07:55.010
You can read the Wikipedia page on it.

02:07:55.010 --> 02:07:58.900
ACH in America still takes three days to clear.

02:07:58.900 --> 02:08:01.409
It's fucking ridiculous.

02:08:01.409 --> 02:08:08.659
But it's because the banks and Visa have too
much power with the financial services committee,

02:08:08.659 --> 02:08:11.551
and they've prevented —
Powell wants to do it.

02:08:11.551 --> 02:08:12.699
It's called FedNow.

02:08:12.699 --> 02:08:17.809
It's been on the books for 10 years, but they
block it because of how we operate.

02:08:17.809 --> 02:08:22.340
The first thing you would do, and no one will
agree to it, is you reverse Citizens United.

02:08:22.340 --> 02:08:24.420
There's too much money in the system.

02:08:24.420 --> 02:08:29.440
You can go to Open Secrets, I think it is,
on almost any one of these decisions and you'll

02:08:29.440 --> 02:08:33.489
see someone — you can just watch the financial
service committee.

02:08:33.489 --> 02:08:38.469
Someone argues against FedNow and then you
look them up and there's a big bank in their

02:08:38.469 --> 02:08:40.409
region, and that big bank's the donor.

02:08:40.409 --> 02:08:46.219
It's not rocket science, but that's where
I would start with some kind of reform.

02:08:46.219 --> 02:08:49.840
One of my first experiences, this would be
definitely be in my TED Talk.

02:08:49.840 --> 02:08:55.320
When one of my first companies I worked on
that had a potential regulatory hurdle, a

02:08:55.320 --> 02:08:59.960
lawyer told me, "Oh, well, you should talk
to these congressmen or whatever.

02:08:59.960 --> 02:09:01.739
Did you want me to introduce you?"

02:09:01.739 --> 02:09:02.739
"Sure."

02:09:02.739 --> 02:09:03.739
Get a phone call.

02:09:03.739 --> 02:09:05.590
"He's going to be in your neighborhood.

02:09:05.590 --> 02:09:08.140
Can you get a bunch of people in a conference
room?"

02:09:08.140 --> 02:09:09.710
I'm like, "What do you mean a bunch of people?

02:09:09.710 --> 02:09:11.090
I just wanted to say hello."

02:09:11.090 --> 02:09:13.850
"No, I need you to get 15 people in the conference
room."

02:09:13.850 --> 02:09:14.850
I go, "Why?"

02:09:14.850 --> 02:09:18.829
He goes, "And they all need to bring the maximum
check that they can donate."

02:09:18.829 --> 02:09:20.130
And I'm like, "Really?"

02:09:20.130 --> 02:09:23.970
I call a few people, I felt horrible.

02:09:23.970 --> 02:09:24.970
"Oh, yeah.

02:09:24.970 --> 02:09:25.970
You've got to give 10 grand.

02:09:25.970 --> 02:09:28.590
I just want to talk to this guy."

02:09:28.590 --> 02:09:32.790
And then a week before they show up, they
go, "Your spouse can give, too.

02:09:32.790 --> 02:09:34.880
Tell everyone their spouse can give."

02:09:34.880 --> 02:09:37.750
This happened to me three times.

02:09:37.750 --> 02:09:38.750
Three times.

02:09:38.750 --> 02:09:40.380
To me, someone in Washington —
Tim Ferriss: Oh, so dirty.

02:09:40.380 --> 02:09:41.380
So dirty.

02:09:41.380 --> 02:09:44.070
Bill Gurley: You need to get 100 grand together
just to —

02:09:44.070 --> 02:09:45.070
Tim Ferriss: Pay to play.

02:09:45.070 --> 02:09:46.070
Bill Gurley: get that first meeting.

02:09:46.070 --> 02:09:47.480
And that that's real.

02:09:47.480 --> 02:09:48.730
That's how —
Tim Ferriss: That's a great speaking fee.

02:09:48.730 --> 02:09:49.840
Bill Gurley: Yeah.

02:09:49.840 --> 02:09:51.690
No, that's how it works.

02:09:51.690 --> 02:09:52.809
And so I don't know.

02:09:52.809 --> 02:09:56.949
You look at things like the healthcare system,
they're just so messed up.

02:09:56.949 --> 02:10:03.290
It's not a free market and it's not single
payer, it's the worst of both, and it's the

02:10:03.290 --> 02:10:04.550
best of from neither.

02:10:04.550 --> 02:10:11.860
Tim Ferriss: If democracy and capitalism are
on the annihilation crash course, I mean,

02:10:11.860 --> 02:10:19.380
what do you think, and I know this is — look,
we're talking about very complex systems and

02:10:19.380 --> 02:10:23.170
all sorts of tertiary effects to who the hell
knows.

02:10:23.170 --> 02:10:26.310
But what do you think the US looks like in
10 or 20 years?

02:10:26.310 --> 02:10:29.640
Bill Gurley: Well, I'll tell you —
Tim Ferriss: And conversely, are there any

02:10:29.640 --> 02:10:30.989
countries that you would be really long on?

02:10:30.989 --> 02:10:31.989
Bill Gurley: That's where I go.

02:10:31.989 --> 02:10:35.080
I mean, despite their current situation that
some people hold them against.

02:10:35.080 --> 02:10:42.770
I think the UK, which is much older than us,
has done a better job than we have.

02:10:42.770 --> 02:10:43.940
One thing they have —
Tim Ferriss: With what?

02:10:43.940 --> 02:10:45.680
Just for clarity, what have they done a better
job with?

02:10:45.680 --> 02:10:49.460
Bill Gurley: I think less regulatory capture.

02:10:49.460 --> 02:10:54.850
And one particular thing they have that is
super clever and will cause every lawyer in

02:10:54.850 --> 02:10:58.400
the world to hate me, they have something
called losing party pays.

02:10:58.400 --> 02:11:01.460
Tim Ferriss: Oh, yes, absolutely.

02:11:01.460 --> 02:11:04.630
I'm astonished that the US functions the way
it does, but yeah, please continue.

02:11:04.630 --> 02:11:11.250
Bill Gurley: And so we live in the United
States of litigation, and a lot of the friction

02:11:11.250 --> 02:11:19.159
that exists that slows down the gears and
messes things up is because of the vigilante

02:11:19.159 --> 02:11:22.040
nature of our legal system.

02:11:22.040 --> 02:11:29.790
And losing party pays just makes the number
of initial litigations filed, dropped by 10X.

02:11:29.790 --> 02:11:33.159
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, losing party pays.

02:11:33.159 --> 02:11:34.170
Yeah.

02:11:34.170 --> 02:11:42.929
Does the UK allow its barristers to have contingency
fees or they must have some type of upper

02:11:42.929 --> 02:11:45.320
limit on that, that is much lower than the
US.

02:11:45.320 --> 02:11:46.320
Bill Gurley: I don’t know.

02:11:46.320 --> 02:11:48.909
Before I give my TED Talk, I'll go a little
deeper.

02:11:48.909 --> 02:11:51.430
Tim Ferriss: All right, regulatory capture.

02:11:51.430 --> 02:11:54.440
Bill, is there anything else you'd like to
say?

02:11:54.440 --> 02:12:00.320
Any closing comments, recommendations, anything
you'd like to point my audience to or ask

02:12:00.320 --> 02:12:01.320
them to do?

02:12:01.320 --> 02:12:05.540
Anything that comes to mind at all that you'd
like to mention before we wind to a close?

02:12:05.540 --> 02:12:06.540
Bill Gurley: I think I'm good, man.

02:12:06.540 --> 02:12:08.400
I think we hit on a lot of different things.

02:12:08.400 --> 02:12:09.710
Tim Ferriss: Yeah, we did.

02:12:09.710 --> 02:12:10.710
We covered a lot.

02:12:10.710 --> 02:12:12.309
And thank you for the time, Bill.

02:12:12.309 --> 02:12:13.309
I really appreciate it.

02:12:13.309 --> 02:12:14.309
Bill Gurley: Thank you.

02:12:14.309 --> 02:12:15.309
Tim Ferriss: This was great.

02:12:15.309 --> 02:12:20.000
And for everybody listening, we will put together
extensive show notes linking to everything

02:12:20.000 --> 02:12:25.020
we talked about, and you can find that as
usual at tim.blog/podcast.

02:12:25.020 --> 02:12:29.420
And until next time, be just a bit kinder
than as necessary.

02:12:29.420 --> 02:12:30.900
And experiment, experiment, experiment.

02:12:30.900 --> 02:12:32.880
Thanks for tuning in.

